/* generated by tools/make-lessons.js from tools/lessons/decisions/*.js; do not edit by hand */
window.LESSONS = Object.assign(window.LESSONS || {}, {"decisions/reliability-diagram":{"idea":"A forecast of 70% is honest if, among all applicants given about 70%, about 70% really repay. A <b>reliability diagram</b> checks this: it sorts forecasts into bins and compares each bin's average forecast with what really happened.","why":"Loan decisions multiply a probability by rupees. If a model says 95% but only 85% repay, every approval is worth less than the model claims, and a cut-off tuned on those claims loses money. AUC cannot warn you: it only looks at the order of applicants, never at whether 0.95 means 95%.","analogy":"A weather app says \"70% chance of rain\". Collect every day it said 70%. If it rained on about 7 of every 10 of those days, the app is honest. If it rained on only 2 of 10, it is over-confident, even if it ranks wetter days above drier days correctly. Days are applicants, rain is repayment, and each group of similar forecasts is a bin.","vocab":[["Calibrated","Among the cases given probability p, a share p really happens, for every p. \"Honest probabilities.\""],["Bin","A group of forecasts in one range, such as 0.8 to 0.9. The page uses 5 to 20 equal-width bins. A forecast exactly on an edge goes into the lower bin."],["Observed share","In one bin: the applicants who repaid divided by all applicants in the bin. This is what <i>really</i> happened."],["Expected calibration error (ECE)","The average gap between forecast and observed share over the bins, each bin weighted by its number of cases: <span class=\"eq\">Σ (n<sub>b</sub> / n) · |observed − forecast|</span>. 0 is perfect."],["Brier score","The mean of (forecast − outcome)² over all applicants, with outcome 1 for repaid and 0 for default. 0 is perfect. Saying 0.5 for everyone scores 0.25."],["Log-loss","The mean of −ln(probability given to what happened). It punishes a confident mistake far harder than the Brier score does."],["Over-confident, under-confident","Over: forecasts more extreme than reality (0.97 said, 0.93 happened). Under: forecasts too timid (0.74 said, 0.88 happened). Neither word says anything about accuracy."]],"steps":[{"h":"A probability cannot be checked on one customer","p":"The model gives a customer 0.93. She repays, or she does not. Neither result proves 0.93 right or wrong. A probability is a promise about a group: of customers like this one, about 93 in 100 repay. So you must check the promise on groups."},{"h":"Make the groups: bins","p":"Cut the range 0 to 1 into equal bins (the page lets you choose 5 to 20). Put each applicant in the bin that holds their forecast, so 0.93 and 0.97 share the bin 0.9 to 1.0. Count the applicants n<sub>b</sub> in each bin. The bars under the diagram show these counts."},{"h":"Two numbers per bin, one dot per bin","p":"For each bin work out the <b>average forecast</b> (what the model promised) and the <b>observed share</b> (repaid divided by n<sub>b</sub>). Plot one dot at (average forecast, observed share). The dot sits at the average forecast of its members, not at the middle of the bin."},{"h":"Read the diagonal","p":"On the dashed diagonal, observed equals promised: honest. Dots that run <b>flatter</b> than the diagonal (above it on the left, below it on the right) mean forecasts that are too extreme: <b>over-confident</b>. Dots steeper than the diagonal mean forecasts that are too timid: <b>under-confident</b>. The slope readout gives this a number: 1 is ideal."},{"h":"ECE: all the gaps as one number","p":"Each bin has a gap, |observed − forecast|. Average the gaps, but weight each bin by its share of the applicants: ECE = Σ (n<sub>b</sub> / n) × gap<sub>b</sub>. A bin with 5 applicants out of 3,000 hardly counts. A bin with 1,200 dominates. The page marks ECE as good below 0.03 and poor above 0.07."},{"h":"Brier score and log-loss: score every applicant","p":"Neither uses bins. Brier is the mean of (forecast − outcome)². Log-loss is the mean of −ln(probability given to the real outcome). Both punish confident mistakes. A 0.97 forecast for an applicant who defaults costs 0.94 in Brier but 3.5 in log-loss, and a forecast of exactly 1 costs about 36, because log-loss clips at about 2×10<sup>−16</sup>."},{"h":"Calibration alone is not enough","p":"Say 0.795 for every applicant (the overall repay rate on the default data). All applicants land in one bin whose forecast equals its observed share, so ECE is about 0. Yet this \"model\" separates nobody. The Brier score exposes it: 0.163, against 0.124 for logistic regression. Read the diagram and ECE for honesty, and the Brier score for usefulness."},{"h":"Bins trade detail for noise","p":"More bins show finer structure but hold fewer applicants each, and the observed share of a small bin jumps around by chance. A gap is never negative, so that noise never cancels and ECE creeps up as bins multiply: logistic regression scores 0.011 with 5 bins and 0.021 with 20. Brier and log-loss do not use bins, so they do not change."}],"example":{"title":"ten customers, five bins, by hand","setup":"A model gave ten customers these probabilities of repaying. A year later you know who repaid (1) and who defaulted (0). Use 5 bins of width 0.2 so the table stays short. Last column: (forecast − outcome)², used for the Brier score.","table":{"head":["Customer","Forecast P(repay)","Repaid?","Bin","(forecast − outcome)²"],"rows":[["1","0.12","0","0 to 0.2","0.0144"],["2","0.35","0","0.2 to 0.4","0.1225"],["3","0.48","1","0.4 to 0.6","0.2704"],["4","0.66","1","0.6 to 0.8","0.1156"],["5","0.74","0","0.6 to 0.8","0.5476"],["6","0.86","1","0.8 to 1.0","0.0196"],["7","0.91","1","0.8 to 1.0","0.0081"],["8","0.93","0","0.8 to 1.0","0.8649"],["9","0.95","1","0.8 to 1.0","0.0025"],["10","0.97","1","0.8 to 1.0","0.0009"]]},"walk":["Count per bin: customers 1, 2 and 3 are alone in their bins, customers 4 and 5 share one, and the other five share the top bin. The weights n<sub>b</sub>/n are 0.1, 0.1, 0.1, 0.2 and 0.5.","Average forecast and observed share per bin: (0.12, 0), (0.35, 0), (0.48, 1), (0.70, 0.50) and (0.924, 0.80). For bin 4: (0.66 + 0.74)/2 = 0.70, and one of two repaid. For bin 5: (0.86 + 0.91 + 0.93 + 0.95 + 0.97)/5 = 4.62/5 = 0.924, and four of five repaid. These five pairs are the five dots.","Gap = |observed − forecast| per bin: 0.12, 0.35, 0.52, 0.20 and 0.124.","<b>ECE</b> = 0.1 × 0.12 + 0.1 × 0.35 + 0.1 × 0.52 + 0.2 × 0.20 + 0.5 × 0.124 = 0.012 + 0.035 + 0.052 + 0.040 + 0.062 = <b>0.201</b>.","<b>Brier</b>: add the last column, 1.9665, and divide by 10: <b>0.197</b>. A lazy forecaster who says 0.6 (the repay rate here) for everyone scores 0.6 × 0.4 = 0.24, so this model beats the lazy one, but not by much.","<b>Log-loss</b> uses −ln of the probability given to what happened. Customer 8 got 0.93 and defaulted: −ln(0.07) = 2.66 (Brier cost 0.865). Customer 9 got 0.95 and repaid: −ln(0.95) = 0.05. The mean over the ten is 0.604.","Check the method against the page: 1-nearest neighbour on the default data forecasts only 0 or 1, so there are two bins. 562 applicants got 0 and 59.8% of them repaid. 2,438 got 1 and 84.1% repaid. ECE = (562/3,000) × 0.598 + (2,438/3,000) × 0.159 = 0.112 + 0.129 = 0.241, the page's value."],"result":"ECE 0.201, Brier 0.197, log-loss 0.604. The big top-bin dot, (0.924, 0.80), lies below the diagonal: the model promised 92% and four of five repaid. Do not read too much into ten customers: one customer more or less moves a one-person bin by a full 1.0. The page uses 3,000 test applicants for this reason, but the arithmetic is exactly the same."},"read":[["A dot","One bin. Across: the average forecast of the applicants in it. Up: the share that really repaid. A bigger dot means more applicants."],["Dashed diagonal","Perfect calibration, where observed share equals forecast. The nearer the dots, the more honest the model."],["Thin line joining the dots","Only a guide for the eye. The readout \"Slope of the dots\" fits one straight line through all dots and lets big bins count more."],["Bars under the diagram","The number of test applicants in each bin. A dot above a tiny bar is noise. The tallest bars decide the ECE."],["Highlighted column and the sentence under the plot","Hover, tap, or click the diagram and press the arrow keys. The sentence names the bin, its cases and the share that repaid, for example \"predicted 0.9 to 1.0: 2,438 cases, 84% really repaid\"."],["A missing dot","A bin with no applicants has no dot. 1-nearest neighbour fills only the first and last bins. The SVM fills nothing above 0.85."],["Verdict box","The page's judgement in words. \"Well calibrated\" needs a slope within 0.15 of 1 and an ECE below 0.04. \"Slightly\" over- or under-confident means a slope within 0.3 of 1 and an ECE below 0.06."],["Readout: Brier score","Mean squared gap between forecast and outcome. Lower is better. The lazy forecaster who says the repay rate for everyone scores about 0.163 on the default data."],["Readout: Log-loss","Lower is better. A probability of exactly 0 or 1 is clipped at about 2×10<sup>−16</sup>, so one confident mistake costs about 36."],["Readout: Expected calibration error","The weighted average gap. The page marks it good below 0.03 and poor above 0.07."],["Readout: Slope of the dots","1 is ideal. Below 1 means over-confident, above 1 under-confident. For two dots it is just rise over run: 1-nearest neighbour gives (0.841 − 0.598) / (1 − 0) = 0.24."],["Controls: Classifier, k, Number of bins, New applicants","k appears only for k-nearest neighbours. Number of bins runs from 5 to 20. New applicants draws a fresh made-up data set: 800 to learn from, 3,000 to test on."]],"experiments":[{"do":"Look at the page as it loads: 1-nearest neighbour, 10 bins (answering <i>Predict first</i> brings you back to this view). Hover over the right-hand dot, or click the diagram and press <i>End</i>.","see":"Only two dots exist. The left one is 562 applicants forecast near 0, and 60% of them repaid. The right one is 2,438 applicants forecast 1, and 84% repaid. ECE 0.241, slope 0.24, log-loss 8.7, verdict \"over-confident\".","why":"One neighbour is a single 0/1 vote, so every forecast is exactly 0 or 1. But the nearest past applicant is only a rough guide to this one: 84% repay, not 100%, and \"0%\" was wrong for 60%. About 724 of the 3,000 forecasts were confidently wrong, and each costs about 36 in log-loss: 724 × 36 / 3,000 = 8.7."},{"do":"Choose <i>Logistic regression</i>.","see":"Ten dots sit on the dashed diagonal. Verdict \"well calibrated\", ECE 0.018, slope 1.08, Brier 0.124, log-loss 0.390. The top bin holds 1,236 applicants forecast 0.95 and 96% of them repaid.","why":"Logistic regression is fitted by minimising log-loss, which pays best when probabilities are honest, and these made-up data really do follow a logistic rule. A model whose shape does not match the data has no such guarantee."},{"do":"Choose <i>Gaussian naive Bayes</i> and hover over the leftmost and the rightmost dot.","see":"Left dot: forecast 0.05, but 33% repaid (above the diagonal). Right dot: forecast 0.97, but 93.5% repaid (below it). Slope 0.62, ECE 0.085, verdict \"over-confident\".","why":"Income, bank balance and savings are three noisy copies of one hidden strength. Naive Bayes treats them as independent and adds their evidence three times, so its log-odds are too large. Its ranking is almost as good as logistic regression's (AUC 0.81 against 0.82; the page does not show AUC), yet its numbers are not honest."},{"do":"Choose <i>k-nearest neighbours</i> and drag <i>k</i> from 1 up to 200.","see":"On the first data set the verdict runs: over-confident up to k = 5, slightly over-confident from about 6, well calibrated from about 11, slightly under-confident from about 25, under-confident from about 37. At k = 200 the slope is 2.05.","why":"A small k builds each forecast from a few votes, so it is jumpy and extreme. A large k averages in neighbours that are not similar, which pulls every forecast towards the overall repay rate of about 80%: too timid. In between, the two errors cancel. After <i>New applicants</i> the k limits shift a little."},{"do":"Choose <i>SVM score through a plain sigmoid</i> and hover over the biggest dot.","see":"No dot above 0.85. The biggest dot holds 2,135 of 3,000 applicants forecast 0.7 to 0.8 (average 0.74), and 88% of them repaid. Verdict \"under-confident\", slope 1.63, ECE 0.112.","why":"An SVM score is a distance from its boundary. Most test scores sit close to +1 (the median is 1.00; three quarters lie between 0.8 and 1.5), and the sigmoid of +1 is 0.73 whatever the truth. Nothing was fitted to tell 0.73 from 0.88. The next simulation fits exactly that correction."},{"do":"Select <i>Logistic regression</i> and move <i>Number of bins</i> to 5, 10 and 20. Then select 1-nearest neighbour and repeat.","see":"Logistic regression: ECE 0.011, 0.018, 0.021. Brier and log-loss do not change at all. For 1-nearest neighbour the ECE stays 0.241 at every setting.","why":"More bins mean fewer applicants per bin, so the observed share jitters by chance and the gap, which is never negative, adds up as error. Brier and log-loss use no bins. 1-nearest neighbour only ever fills the bins for 0 and 1, so slicing the range finer changes nothing."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Picture","text":"Dots on the diagonal are honest. <b>Flatter than the diagonal is over-confident</b>: the model shouts \"0.97\" and reality says 0.93. Steeper than the diagonal is under-confident: it whispers \"0.74\" and reality says 0.88."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"Look at the bars before the dots. A dot with a tiny bar under it is noise. The biggest bar usually decides the ECE, so ask \"how many cases?\" before \"how far from the diagonal?\"."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"The lazy forecaster who says the overall repay rate ȳ for everyone has an ECE of about 0 and a Brier score of ȳ(1 − ȳ), which is 0.163 on the default data. A real model must beat that Brier score, so never judge ECE alone."},{"tag":"Formula in words","text":"<b>ECE</b> = average gap, weighted by how many cases. <b>Brier</b> = average squared miss. <b>Log-loss</b> = average surprise at what really happened."},{"tag":"Trick","text":"AUC hides dishonesty, because it only sees order. Naive Bayes has AUC 0.81 against 0.82 for logistic regression, yet ECE 0.085 against 0.018. Draw the diagram for your own model; never assume."},{"tag":"Link to code","text":"<code>calibration_curve(y_true, y_prob)</code> returns the <i>observed share first</i> and the <i>mean forecast second</i>. Writing <code>mean_pred, frac_pos = ...</code> swaps the axes: a classic slip."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"The model said 90% and the applicant defaulted, so the model was wrong.\"","right":"A 90% forecast should fail 1 time in 10. One case proves nothing. Check the whole bin: of the applicants forecast about 90%, did about 90% repay?"},{"wrong":"\"Over-confident means the model is very accurate.\"","right":"It means the forecasts are more extreme than what happens. 1-nearest neighbour says 100% for 2,438 applicants and only 84% of them repay."},{"wrong":"\"A high AUC means the probabilities are good.\"","right":"AUC uses only the order of the forecasts. Any increasing change to the numbers leaves it alone but can wreck calibration. Check the diagram as well."},{"wrong":"\"An ECE of zero means a great model.\"","right":"Saying the overall repay rate (0.795) for every applicant gives an ECE of about 0 and no skill at all. Pair ECE with the Brier score."},{"wrong":"\"More bins give a more accurate ECE.\"","right":"More bins leave fewer cases per bin, so chance adds to every gap and ECE drifts upward even for an honest model. Compare ECE only at the same number of bins."}],"code":[{"title":"Reliability table, ECE and Brier score by hand (the worked example)","py":"import numpy as np\np = np.array([0.12, 0.35, 0.48, 0.66, 0.74, 0.86, 0.91, 0.93, 0.95, 0.97])  # forecast P(repay)\ny = np.array([0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 1, 1, 0, 1, 1])                                # 1 = repaid\nb = np.searchsorted([0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8], p)     # bin number 0..4 for each customer\nece = 0\nfor k in range(5):\n    m = b == k                                   # True for the customers in bin k\n    if m.any():                                  # skip empty bins\n        print(k, m.sum(), round(p[m].mean(), 3), round(y[m].mean(), 3))\n        ece += m.mean() * abs(p[m].mean() - y[m].mean())   # m.mean() is n_b / n\nprint(\"ECE\", round(ece, 3), \"Brier\", round(np.mean((p - y) ** 2), 3))","out":"0 1 0.12 0.0\n1 1 0.35 0.0\n2 1 0.48 1.0\n3 2 0.7 0.5\n4 5 0.924 0.8\nECE 0.201 Brier 0.197","walk":[["np.searchsorted([0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8], p)","For each forecast, counts the bin edges below it, which is its bin number 0 to 4. A forecast exactly on an edge stays in the lower bin, as on the page. Four inner edges make five bins."],["m = b == k","A True/False array with one entry per customer: True where the customer is in bin k. Use it as an index, <code>p[m]</code>, to pick that bin's forecasts."],["m.any()","False for an empty bin. Skipping it avoids taking the mean of nothing, which would give NaN."],["p[m].mean(), y[m].mean()","The dot of the bin: average forecast, and observed share. Because y is 0 or 1, its mean is the share that repaid."],["m.mean()","The mean of True/False values is the fraction of customers in the bin, n<sub>b</sub>/n. That is the weight in the ECE formula."],["np.mean((p - y) ** 2)","The Brier score: square each miss, then average. <code>**</code> is the power operator."]],"tip":"sklearn.metrics.brier_score_loss(y, p) gives the same Brier score. np.searchsorted and the page both use \"lower bin on an edge\"; np.digitize would put the edge case in the upper bin."},{"title":"Same ranking, different honesty: logistic regression against naive Bayes","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nfrom sklearn.naive_bayes import GaussianNB\nfrom sklearn.metrics import roc_auc_score, brier_score_loss\nfrom sklearn.calibration import calibration_curve\nrng = np.random.RandomState(0); s = rng.normal(size=4000)       # hidden \"financial strength\"\nX = s[:, None] + 0.5 * rng.normal(size=(4000, 3))               # three noisy copies of it\ny = (rng.uniform(size=4000) < 1 / (1 + np.exp(-(1.4 + 1.2 * s)))).astype(int)   # 1 = repaid\nfor m in (LogisticRegression(), GaussianNB()):\n    p = m.fit(X[:2000], y[:2000]).predict_proba(X[2000:])[:, 1]  # learn on half, score the other half\n    f, c = calibration_curve(y[2000:], p, n_bins=10)             # f: observed share, c: mean forecast\n    print(type(m).__name__, round(roc_auc_score(y[2000:], p), 3), round(brier_score_loss(y[2000:], p), 3), round(np.abs(f - c).max(), 2))","out":"LogisticRegression 0.775 0.153 0.09\nGaussianNB 0.776 0.178 0.32","walk":[["rng = np.random.RandomState(0)","A random generator with a fixed seed, so every run makes the same data. <code>s</code> is one hidden strength number per applicant."],["s[:, None] + 0.5 * rng.normal(size=(4000, 3))","<code>s[:, None]</code> turns 4,000 numbers into a column of shape (4000, 1). Adding a (4000, 3) noise table copies that column across 3 columns: three noisy measurements of the same strength, like income, balance and savings."],["y = (rng.uniform(...) < 1 / (1 + np.exp(...))).astype(int)","Each applicant repays with probability sigmoid(1.4 + 1.2 s). The comparison gives True/False, and <code>astype(int)</code> turns it into 1/0."],["m.fit(X[:2000], y[:2000])","Learn from the first 2,000 rows. <code>X[2000:]</code> is held out for scoring."],["predict_proba(...)[:, 1]","Returns two columns, P(class 0) and P(class 1). <code>[:, 1]</code> keeps P(repay) because the classes are sorted 0 then 1."],["calibration_curve(y_true, p, n_bins=10)","Returns the observed share and mean forecast of each non-empty bin, in that order. The last number printed is the biggest gap among the bins."]],"tip":"Both models have almost the same AUC (0.775 and 0.776) because they rank applicants alike. The Brier score and the worst bin gap are what separate them."}],"check":[{"q":"A model gave 400 applicants a forecast of 0.90. Of them, 300 repaid. What is the verdict for this bin?","options":["Well calibrated: 300 of 400 is a high number","Over-confident: forecast 0.90, observed 0.75","Cannot say, because a bin needs 1,000 cases","Under-confident: forecast 0.90, observed 0.75"],"answer":1,"why":"Observed share = 300/400 = 0.75, which is below the 0.90 promised. Fewer repaid than promised means the forecast was too extreme: over-confident, by 0.15 in this bin."},{"q":"Two bins. Bin A: 200 applicants, average forecast 0.2, observed share 0.3. Bin B: 800 applicants, average forecast 0.8, observed share 0.7. What is the ECE?","options":["0.10","0.20","0.00","0.05"],"answer":0,"why":"Gaps are 0.1 and 0.1. Weights are 200/1,000 = 0.2 and 800/1,000 = 0.8. ECE = 0.2 × 0.1 + 0.8 × 0.1 = 0.10. The two errors have opposite signs (above and below the diagonal) but the absolute value stops them cancelling."},{"q":"You keep a model's forecasts the same and change the page's <i>Number of bins</i> from 10 to 20. What happens?","options":["Both Brier and ECE halve","Both Brier and ECE stay the same","The Brier score rises; ECE stays the same","The Brier score stays the same; ECE usually rises a little"],"answer":3,"why":"The Brier score is computed per applicant with no bins, so it cannot change. With 20 bins each holds fewer applicants, so chance moves the observed shares more, and the sum of absolute gaps tends to grow."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain to a friend how a reliability diagram tells you whether a model's 70% means 70%.","model":"Group the applicants by the forecast the model gave (bins), then for each group compare the average forecast with the share that really repaid. If those two numbers match, so the dots sit on the diagonal, the model's percentages can be taken at face value; dots flatter than the diagonal mean it is over-confident."},"cheat":"Calibrated: of cases forecast p, a share p happen. Reliability diagram: one dot per bin at (average forecast, observed share); the diagonal is honest, flatter is over-confident. ECE = Σ (n_b/n)|observed − forecast|. Brier = mean (forecast − outcome)²; log-loss punishes confident mistakes harder. Zero ECE alone proves nothing: always check Brier too."},"decisions/calibration-fix":{"idea":"A calibrator is a small correction, learned on spare rows, that turns a model's raw score into an honest probability. <b>Platt scaling</b> fits an S-curve with two numbers. <b>Isotonic regression</b> fits a staircase that never goes down. Neither swaps two applicants.","why":"Some models rank well but report dishonest numbers: an SVM score has no probability meaning, and naive Bayes is too extreme. Cost decisions need honest probabilities (next simulation). Retraining rarely fixes it. A calibrator repairs the output with a few hundred spare rows and leaves the model untouched.","analogy":"An over-excited weather app. Take last season's forecasts and what actually happened, and write a correction rule: \"when the app says 90%, say 78%\". A formula with two numbers that bends every forecast the same way is Platt scaling. A table of corrections by forecast range, never letting a higher forecast get a lower value, is isotonic regression. Write the rule on one season, check it on another.","vocab":[["Score","The raw number a model produces before any correction: log-odds for logistic regression and naive Bayes, a distance from the boundary for an SVM."],["Calibration set","Spare rows with known outcomes that the model was <i>not</i> trained on. Used only to learn the correction."],["Platt scaling","<span class=\"eq\">p = 1 / (1 + e<sup>−(a·score + b)</sup>)</span>. Logistic regression whose only input is the score. Two numbers, a and b."],["Isotonic regression","A non-decreasing step function fitted to the calibration rows: a higher score never gets a lower probability. No other shape is assumed."],["Pool adjacent violators","The algorithm behind isotonic regression: when a block's average is above the next block's, merge the two into their weighted average. Repeat until the averages never go down."],["Flat step","One level of the isotonic staircase. The readout \"Isotonic: flat steps\" counts them. More steps means more freedom and more risk with few rows."],["Increasing map","A correction that never reverses the order of two scores, so it cannot swap two applicants."]],"steps":[{"h":"The score is not yet a probability","p":"Each model gives a raw score. Pushing it through <span class=\"eq\">1 / (1 + e<sup>−score</sup>)</span> gives a number between 0 and 1 that <i>looks</i> like a probability, but nothing has checked it. For the SVM on the default data, about three quarters of the test scores lie between 0.8 and 1.5, which the sigmoid turns into 0.69 to 0.82. Most applicants get nearly the same \"probability\"."},{"h":"Learn the correction from examples","p":"Take rows the model has not seen, with known outcomes. For each, record the model's score and whether the applicant repaid. Then fit a function g that maps a score to the repay rate among rows with that score. If g only goes up, the order of applicants is kept. Only the numbers change."},{"h":"Platt scaling: an S-curve with two knobs","p":"<span class=\"eq\">p = 1 / (1 + e<sup>−(a·score + b)</sup>)</span>. This is logistic regression with the score as its only feature, so it is quick and stable. The slope a stretches or shrinks the score: a below 1 shrinks over-confident log-odds (naive Bayes gets a = 0.33 on the default data), a above 1 stretches timid ones (the SVM gets 1.72). The intercept b slides the curve. a = 1 and b = 0 means no correction was needed."},{"h":"Isotonic regression: only \"higher score, not lower probability\"","p":"Sort the calibration rows by score. Start with each row as its own block, with value 1 (repaid) or 0 (defaulted). Whenever a block's average is above the next block's, pool the two into their weighted average. Repeat until the averages never decrease. The result is a staircase. scikit-learn joins neighbouring steps with straight ramps, as the page does."},{"h":"Few knobs against many knobs","p":"Platt has 2 numbers, so 50 rows are enough, but it can only draw an S. Isotonic has as many steps as the data support, so it can follow any rising shape, but with 100 rows each step rests on a handful of customers and the ends reach exactly 0 or 1. SVM, 30 datasets: isotonic had the worse Brier score on 22 datasets with 50 rows, 19 with 100, 15 with 200, 8 with 500, 2 with 1,000 and none with 2,000."},{"h":"Fit on rows the model has not seen","p":"On its own training rows a model looks better than it will on new customers, because it was tuned to those rows. A calibrator fitted there learns a flattering picture and corrects too little (isotonic simply memorises the rows). Use a separate calibration set, or cross-fit as <code>CalibratedClassifierCV</code> does. Keep the test rows out too: they are for the final check only."},{"h":"Calibration repairs honesty, not ranking","p":"Platt's curve rises, so no two applicants swap and the AUC is exactly unchanged. Isotonic merges neighbours into ties, and a tie counts half a point, so its AUC can move a little in either direction. A calibrator cannot turn a poor ranker into a good one. With 50 rows Platt's slope a even came out zero or negative on 2 of 30 SVM datasets, which reverses the ranking."}],"example":{"title":"ten calibration rows, pooled by hand","setup":"An SVM gave ten calibration customers these scores s, and you know who repaid (1) and who defaulted (0). They are already sorted by score. Fit an isotonic staircase by hand, then compare it with Platt scaling (a and b come from the computer: Newton's method is not a hand job).","table":{"head":["Row","Score s","Repaid y","Raw sigmoid(s)","Isotonic","Platt p"],"rows":[["1","−1.2","0","0.231","0","0.058"],["2","−0.8","0","0.310","0","0.124"],["3","−0.5","1","0.378","0.333","0.207"],["4","−0.2","0","0.450","0.333","0.326"],["5","0.1","0","0.525","0.333","0.472"],["6","0.3","1","0.574","0.5","0.574"],["7","0.5","0","0.622","0.5","0.670"],["8","0.8","1","0.690","1","0.790"],["9","1.0","1","0.731","1","0.850"],["10","1.4","1","0.802","1","0.928"]]},"walk":["Start with every row as its own block, values 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1, 1, 1. Scan left to right and look for a block that is higher than the one on its right.","Row 3 (value 1) is followed by row 4 (value 0): the averages go down, which isotonic forbids. Pool them: (1 + 0)/2 = 0.5, a block of two rows.","That 0.5 is now above row 5 (value 0). Pool again: (1 + 0 + 0)/3 = 0.333, a block of three rows (rows 3 to 5).","Row 6 (value 1) is followed by row 7 (value 0). Pool: 0.5 for rows 6 and 7. Since 0.333 is below 0.5, there is no further violation. Rows 8 to 10 are all 1.","Result: 0, 0, 0.333, 0.333, 0.333, 0.5, 0.5, 1, 1, 1. That is <b>4 flat steps</b> (levels 0, 0.333, 0.5 and 1), as the readout \"Isotonic: flat steps\" would say.","Platt on the same rows gives a = 2.054 and b = −0.317. For a new customer with s = 0.2: a·s + b = 0.411 − 0.317 = 0.094, and <span class=\"eq\">1 / (1 + e<sup>−0.094</sup>)</span> = <b>0.523</b>. The raw sigmoid of 0.2 gives 0.550.","Isotonic at s = 0.2: the nearest step ends are (0.1, 0.333) and (0.3, 0.5). A straight ramp joins them, and 0.2 is halfway, so <b>0.417</b>."],"result":"One customer, three answers: raw 0.55, Platt 0.52, isotonic 0.42. With 10 rows the staircase fits these very rows best (Brier 0.117 on them, against 0.168 for Platt and 0.179 for the raw sigmoid), and it says exactly 1 for any score above 0.8 because three customers repaid. That is memory, not knowledge. Judge a calibrator only on rows it did not learn from: on the page with 100 rows, isotonic scores 0.1454 against Platt's 0.1382 on 3,000 fresh test applicants."},"read":[["Table \"Scores on the test applicants\"","Brier, Log-loss, Calib. error and AUC for the Raw model, after Platt and after Isotonic, measured on 3,000 held-out applicants. Lower is better in the first three columns. The best value in each column is highlighted."],["Reliability chart: triangles, circles, diamonds","Triangles on a dashed line: \"raw model\". Circles: \"after Platt scaling\". Diamonds: \"after isotonic regression\". The nearer to the dashed diagonal, the more honest. The bars count the raw forecasts per bin."],["Maps plot: dashed curve","\"plain sigmoid of the score\": what the raw model does. Horizontal axis: model score. Vertical axis: probability."],["Maps plot: smooth S-curve","\"Platt scaling\": <span class=\"eq\">sigmoid(a·score + b)</span>, fitted to the calibration rows."],["Maps plot: staircase","\"isotonic regression\": it only rises, and climbs where repayers outnumber defaulters among neighbouring scores."],["Short ticks on the top and bottom edges of the maps plot","Calibration rows (the first 700 are drawn). Top edge: repaid. Bottom edge: defaulted. Where both edges have ticks, the score cannot tell the two apart."],["Readouts: Platt: slope a, Platt: intercept b","The two fitted numbers. Slope a above 1 stretches the score, below 1 shrinks it."],["Readout: Isotonic: flat steps","The number of distinct levels in the staircase."],["Slider: Rows in the calibration set","50 to 2,000 fresh applicants. Both calibrators learn from these rows only, and are scored on the same 3,000 test applicants."],["Checkbox: Calibrate on the same rows the model was trained on","Uses up to 800 training rows instead of fresh ones. This is the mistake the lesson warns about."],["Plot: Repeat on 30 new datasets","Average test score against calibration rows over 30 datasets. The Score control switches between Brier score, Calibration error and Log-loss. The dashed horizontal line is the raw model. The sentence below counts datasets where isotonic did worse than Platt."],["Controls: Classifier, New data","Three models to fix: the SVM score, Gaussian naive Bayes and 5-nearest neighbours. New data redraws everything."]],"experiments":[{"do":"Read the table at the defaults (SVM score, 100 calibration rows).","see":"Calib. error: raw 0.107, Platt 0.054, isotonic 0.085. Brier: 0.1501, 0.1382, 0.1454. Platt slope a = 1.72, intercept b = −0.25.","why":"The plain sigmoid squeezes the SVM's scores into a narrow band around 0.73. Platt's a = 1.72 stretches them back out, so the forecasts spread over 0.1 to 0.9 and match what happens. Isotonic has to discover the same shape from only 100 rows and ends up noisier."},{"do":"Drag <i>Rows in the calibration set</i> from 50 up to 2,000.","see":"Brier (Platt, isotonic): 0.1383 and 0.1487 at 50 rows, 0.1367 and 0.1346 at 2,000. Calibration error: Platt 0.058 to 0.049, isotonic 0.095 to 0.021. Flat steps: 5 at 50 rows, 8 at 100, 17 at 2,000.","why":"Platt has two numbers, so it settles quickly, but an S-curve is not exactly the SVM's shape and it stops improving near 0.05. Isotonic starts worse because few rows give a jumpy staircase, but its steps are free, so with enough rows it follows the true shape."},{"do":"Read the <i>Log-loss</i> column at 50, 100 and 2,000 rows, and watch the ends of the isotonic staircase in the maps plot.","see":"Isotonic log-loss: 1.243, 0.958 and 0.531. The raw model is 0.480 and Platt stays near 0.44. At every size the staircase starts at exactly 0 and ends at exactly 1.","why":"The lowest scores are all defaulters and the highest all repayers, so the pooled blocks at the two ends are pure 0 or 1. A forecast of exactly 0 or 1 that is wrong costs about 36 in log-loss, so a few misses ruin the average. On this data isotonic never beats the raw log-loss."},{"do":"Compare the <i>AUC</i> column across the three rows, at 100 rows and at 2,000 rows.","see":"Raw and Platt show the same AUC, 0.7215, at every size. Isotonic shows 0.7122 at 100 rows and 0.7323 at 2,000.","why":"Platt is an increasing curve, so it keeps every pair in order. Isotonic turns neighbouring scores into ties, and a tie counts half a point. A tie can lower the AUC (a correctly ordered pair becomes a tie) or raise it (a wrongly ordered pair becomes a tie), so it can drift either way."},{"do":"Drag <i>Rows in the calibration set</i> to 1,000 (only 800 training rows exist, so the page uses 800), then tick <i>Calibrate on the same rows the model was trained on</i>. Then switch the classifier to naive Bayes.","see":"SVM: Platt's calibration error rises to 0.083 and isotonic's to 0.156, worse than the uncorrected 0.107. Platt's slope jumps to 4.08. Naive Bayes: Platt 0.019 and isotonic 0.018 on the training rows, against 0.020 and 0.011 on 1,000 fresh rows.","why":"The SVM has been fitted to those 800 rows, so its scores separate them much better than they separate new customers. Platt therefore learns \"scores are very informative\" (a = 4.08) and over-corrects. Naive Bayes has only about a dozen numbers to fit, so it cannot memorise its training rows."},{"do":"With the SVM selected, press <i>Repeat on 30 new datasets</i> (it also runs once by itself when the page first loads; switching classifier clears it), and use the <i>Score</i> control to switch between Brier score, Calibration error and Log-loss.","see":"Brier: the two lines meet near 200 rows (0.137 each) and isotonic is lower from 500 rows. Calibration error: isotonic is lower from 200 rows (0.044 against 0.045). Log-loss: Platt stays lower at every size (0.435 against 0.457 at 2,000 rows).","why":"Averaging over 30 datasets removes the luck of a single draw. Isotonic wins on how close it follows reality once rows are plentiful, but its exact 0 and 1 keep its log-loss behind. The sentence under the plot counts the datasets where isotonic did worse: 19 of 30 at 100 rows, 0 of 30 at 2,000."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Picture","text":"Platt is a <b>dial</b>: two knobs, a (stretch) and b (shift), one smooth S. Isotonic is a <b>staircase</b>: as many steps as the data support, never down. Few rows, use the dial. Many rows, the staircase."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"Under about 200 calibration rows, or when the model is naive Bayes, start with Platt. From about 1,000 rows, isotonic usually wins on Brier and calibration error. Between, try both and compare on rows neither has seen."},{"tag":"Trick","text":"Read Platt's a as a verdict. <b>a below 1</b>: the model shouted, shrink it (naive Bayes 0.33). <b>a above 1</b>: it whispered, stretch it (SVM 1.72). a near 1 and b near 0: leave it alone."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"After calibrating, the AUC must not fall for Platt (it is a rising curve). If Platt's AUC changed, the fitted a was zero or negative, which usually means too few calibration rows."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"Never score a calibrator on the rows it learned from. Isotonic looks best there because it can memorise them: 0.117 against 0.168 for Platt on the ten example rows."},{"tag":"Link to code","text":"<code>CalibratedClassifierCV(model, method=\"sigmoid\", cv=5)</code>. \"sigmoid\" is scikit-learn's name for Platt scaling. <code>cv=5</code> cross-fits so every calibration row is unseen by the copy of the model that scores it."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"Calibration makes the model more accurate.\"","right":"It changes the probabilities, not the ranking. Platt scaling leaves the AUC exactly where it was (0.7215 before and after on the default data)."},{"wrong":"\"Fit the calibrator on the training rows: they are the data we have.\"","right":"The model has already learned those rows. For the SVM, isotonic on the 800 training rows gave an average calibration error of 0.165 over 30 datasets, against 0.032 with 500 fresh rows."},{"wrong":"\"Isotonic is more flexible, so it is always better.\"","right":"Flexibility needs rows. With 100 rows isotonic had the worse Brier score on 19 of 30 SVM datasets, and for naive Bayes Platt won on at least 27 of 30 at every size from 50 to 2,000 rows."},{"wrong":"\"Platt scaling is only for SVMs.\"","right":"It was invented for SVM scores but works on any score. For naive Bayes with 100 rows it took the average Brier score from 0.146 to 0.127."},{"wrong":"\"I can fit the calibrator on the test rows too.\"","right":"Then no rows are left to measure honestly whether the correction works. Keep three sets apart: train the model, fit the calibrator, test the result."}],"code":[{"title":"Fit both calibrators on ten rows (the worked example)","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.isotonic import IsotonicRegression\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\ns = np.array([-1.2, -0.8, -0.5, -0.2, 0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.8, 1.0, 1.4])   # model scores of 10 calibration rows\ny = np.array([0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1, 1, 1])                           # 1 = repaid\niso = IsotonicRegression(out_of_bounds=\"clip\").fit(s, y)               # the staircase\nprint(\"isotonic:\", iso.predict(s).round(3).tolist(), \"at 0.2:\", round(float(iso.predict([0.2])[0]), 3))\nplatt = LogisticRegression(C=1e10).fit(s.reshape(-1, 1), y)            # the S-curve; huge C switches off shrinkage\na, b = platt.coef_[0, 0], platt.intercept_[0]\nprint(\"Platt: a =\", round(a, 3), \"b =\", round(b, 3), \"at 0.2:\", round(1 / (1 + np.exp(-(a * 0.2 + b))), 3))","out":"isotonic: [0.0, 0.0, 0.333, 0.333, 0.333, 0.5, 0.5, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0] at 0.2: 0.417\nPlatt: a = 2.054 b = -0.317 at 0.2: 0.523","walk":[["IsotonicRegression(out_of_bounds=\"clip\")","The staircase fitter. <code>out_of_bounds=\"clip\"</code> means a score beyond the training range gets the nearest end value instead of an error."],[".fit(s, y)","Learns from scores first, outcomes second. s is a plain 1-D array because isotonic regression has one input."],["iso.predict(s)","The fitted staircase values at the calibration scores: the \"Isotonic\" column of the table. Between steps it interpolates, which is why 0.2 gives 0.417."],["LogisticRegression(C=1e10)","Platt scaling is logistic regression. <code>C</code> is the inverse of the shrinkage strength; a huge C removes shrinkage, so you get the plain maximum-likelihood a and b that the page fits."],["s.reshape(-1, 1)","scikit-learn models want a 2-D table (rows by features). <code>-1</code> means \"work out the number of rows\"; <code>1</code> means one feature, the score."],["platt.coef_[0, 0], platt.intercept_[0]","Fitted attributes end in an underscore. <code>coef_</code> is a table with one row per class boundary and one column per feature, so [0, 0] is a. <code>intercept_</code> holds b."]],"tip":"The Platt value is computed by hand from a and b so you see there is no magic: it is just 1 / (1 + exp(−(a·s + b)))."},{"title":"Calibrate naive Bayes the practical way, with cross-fitting","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.naive_bayes import GaussianNB\nfrom sklearn.calibration import CalibratedClassifierCV\nfrom sklearn.metrics import brier_score_loss\nrng = np.random.RandomState(0); s = rng.normal(size=4000)          # hidden strength\nX = s[:, None] + 0.5 * rng.normal(size=(4000, 3))                  # three noisy copies: naive Bayes counts it 3 times\ny = (rng.uniform(size=4000) < 1 / (1 + np.exp(-(1.4 + 1.2 * s)))).astype(int)\nfor method in (None, \"sigmoid\", \"isotonic\"):                       # scikit-learn calls Platt scaling \"sigmoid\"\n    m = GaussianNB() if method is None else CalibratedClassifierCV(GaussianNB(), method=method, cv=5)\n    p = m.fit(X[:2000], y[:2000]).predict_proba(X[2000:])[:, 1]    # fit on rows 0-1999, score rows 2000-3999\n    print(method, round(brier_score_loss(y[2000:], p), 3))","out":"None 0.178\nsigmoid 0.155\nisotonic 0.154","walk":[["GaussianNB()","The uncalibrated model. Its probabilities are too extreme because the three columns repeat the same evidence."],["CalibratedClassifierCV(GaussianNB(), method=..., cv=5)","A wrapper that does the whole job. With <code>cv=5</code> it splits the training rows in 5 folds. For each fold it trains the model on 4/5 of the rows and fits the calibrator on the remaining 1/5, so calibration rows are always unseen."],["method=\"sigmoid\"","Platt scaling. <code>method=\"isotonic\"</code> gives the staircase."],[".fit(X[:2000], y[:2000])","Everything here uses only the first 2,000 rows. The wrapper handles the splitting inside them."],[".predict_proba(X[2000:])[:, 1]","Probabilities for the held-out rows, averaged over the 5 calibrated copies. <code>[:, 1]</code> keeps P(repay)."],["brier_score_loss(y_true, p)","The Brier score: lower is better. Calibration cut it from 0.178 to about 0.155, while the AUC (not printed) stays 0.776."]],"tip":"With cv=5 you never have to choose a separate calibration set yourself, which is why this is the usual way to do it in practice."},{"title":"Why the calibration rows must be fresh","py":"from sklearn.datasets import make_classification\nfrom sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier as RF\nfrom sklearn.isotonic import IsotonicRegression as Iso\nfrom sklearn.metrics import brier_score_loss as brier\nX, y = make_classification(n_samples=6000, n_features=10, n_informative=4, flip_y=0.15, class_sep=0.8, random_state=0)\ntr, cal, te = slice(0, 1500), slice(1500, 3000), slice(3000, 6000)    # three disjoint blocks of rows\nrf = RF(n_estimators=100, random_state=0).fit(X[tr], y[tr])\np = rf.predict_proba(X)[:, 1]                                          # the forest's raw P(class 1) for every row\nfor name, rows in [(\"same rows\", tr), (\"fresh rows\", cal)]:\n    iso = Iso(out_of_bounds=\"clip\").fit(p[rows], y[rows])\n    print(name, round(brier(y[te], iso.predict(p[te])), 3))\nprint(\"no calibration\", round(brier(y[te], p[te]), 3))","out":"same rows 0.184\nfresh rows 0.144\nno calibration 0.145","walk":[["slice(0, 1500)","A reusable \"rows 0 to 1,499\". <code>X[tr]</code> then picks those rows. Three slices give three disjoint blocks: train, calibration, test."],["RF(n_estimators=100, ...)","A random forest, which fits its training rows almost perfectly. That is what makes its training-row forecasts misleading."],["p = rf.predict_proba(X)[:, 1]","The forest's raw probability for every row, computed once. On the <code>tr</code> rows these are over-sure because the forest has seen those rows."],["Iso(...).fit(p[rows], y[rows])","Learn the staircase from forecasts and outcomes of the chosen rows: first the training rows, then the fresh ones."],["iso.predict(p[te])","Apply the staircase to the test forecasts, and score it with the Brier score against the real outcomes <code>y[te]</code>."]],"tip":"On the same rows the staircase learns \"the forest is nearly perfect\" and corrects almost nothing useful (0.184); on fresh rows it learns the truth (0.144, slightly better than the raw 0.145)."}],"check":[{"q":"Why must a calibrator be fitted on rows the model was <i>not</i> trained on?","options":["scikit-learn refuses to run otherwise","Platt scaling needs rows without labels","On its own training rows the model looks better than it will on new customers, so the calibrator learns a flattering picture","Training rows change the order of the applicants"],"answer":2,"why":"The model has been tuned to its training rows, so its scores there separate repayers from defaulters better than they will for new applicants. A calibrator fitted on them corrects too little (or memorises them, for isotonic)."},{"q":"Naive Bayes gives a customer a raw log-odds score of 2.0, so the plain sigmoid says 0.88. Platt scaling fits a = 0.5 and b = 0. What probability does the calibrated model give?","options":["0.73","0.62","0.50","0.88"],"answer":0,"why":"a·score + b = 0.5 × 2.0 + 0 = 1.0, and 1 / (1 + e^−1) = 0.73. Because a is below 1, the over-confident 0.88 is pulled back towards 0.5."},{"q":"You have only 60 calibration rows and can use either calibrator. What do you expect?","options":["Platt is usually safer: isotonic would build steps on a few customers each and may hit exactly 0 or 1","They give identical results below 100 rows","Isotonic is safer because it makes no assumptions","Neither works below 100 rows"],"answer":0,"why":"With so few rows isotonic's steps rest on tiny groups, so it follows noise and reaches exactly 0 or 1, which hurts log-loss badly. Platt's two numbers can be estimated stably from 60 rows. On the SVM data isotonic had the worse Brier score on 22 of 30 datasets with 50 rows."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain what a calibrator learns and why it must be fitted on rows the model has not seen.","model":"A calibrator learns a map from the model's raw score to the share of applicants who really repay at that score, using rows with known outcomes; Platt scaling fits an S-curve with two numbers and isotonic regression a rising staircase. It must use rows the model has not seen, because on its training rows the model looks better than it will on new customers and the calibrator would learn that flattering picture."},"cheat":"Calibrator = map from raw score to honest probability, learned on unseen rows. Platt: p = 1/(1+e^−(a·score+b)), two numbers, best with few rows or S-shaped error. Isotonic: rising staircase by pooling adjacent violators, best with 1,000+ rows. Platt keeps AUC exactly; isotonic may create ties. Never fit or score on the rows the model learned from."},"decisions/cost-threshold":{"idea":"Approve a loan when its expected profit is above zero: <span class=\"eq\">p·G − (1 − p)·L &gt; 0</span>. Solving for p gives the cut-off <b>L / (G + L)</b>. With +1,000 and −9,000 that is 0.90, not 0.5, provided p is honest.","why":"Accuracy treats all mistakes alike, but here one default wipes out nine repaid loans. On 3,000 made-up applicants a cut-off of 0.5 approves 94% of them and loses 1,642,000. The cut-off from the costs, 0.90, earns 797,000 with the same model. The cut-off is a money decision, not a default setting.","analogy":"A shopkeeper sells on credit. A customer who pays earns him 1,000. A customer who does not pay costs him 9,000 of goods. Nine good customers only just pay for one bad one. So he should give credit only when he is more than 9 times out of 10 sure the customer will pay. \"Probably pays\" is not enough.","vocab":[["Gain G, loss L","G is the money earned when you approve a loan that is repaid (1,000). L is the money lost when you approve a loan that defaults (9,000). Rejecting earns 0."],["Expected profit","The average result if you could make the same decision many times. For approving an applicant with repay chance p: <span class=\"eq\">p·G − (1 − p)·L</span>."],["Cut-off (threshold)","Approve when P(repay) is at least this number. The page calls it \"Approve if P(repay) is at least\"."],["Break-even probability","The p where expected profit is exactly 0. Solving gives <span class=\"eq\">L / (G + L)</span>. It is the right cut-off."],["Calibrated probability","A P(repay) that is honest: of applicants given 0.90, about 90% repay. The formula assumes it."],["Profit curve","Total profit on a set of applicants if you approve everyone at or above each cut-off in turn. Its peak is the best cut-off <i>for that set</i>."],["Cost matrix","The 2 × 2 table of money: approve or reject, against repaid or defaulted. Here +1,000, −9,000, 0 and 0."]],"steps":[{"h":"Accuracy is the wrong score","p":"At a cut-off of 0.5 the model on the page is right for 83.4% of the 3,000 applicants and still loses 1,642,000. Counting right answers treats \"approved a default\" and \"rejected a good customer\" as equal. They are not: one costs 9,000 and the other costs only the 1,000 you did not earn. What you need is money per decision."},{"h":"Write the money for every decision","p":"Four cells. Approve and the loan is repaid: +G. Approve and it defaults: −L. Reject: 0, whatever would have happened. The page shows this as the \"Decisions against outcomes\" table, with the money in each cell and the total at the bottom."},{"h":"Expected profit of approving one applicant","p":"The model says the chance of repayment is p. Over many applicants like this one, a share p repay and earn G, and a share 1 − p default and lose L. Average money per approval: <span class=\"eq\">p·G − (1 − p)·L</span>. Rejecting gives 0, so approving only wins when this is above 0."},{"h":"Solve for p","p":"<span class=\"eq\">p·G − (1 − p)·L &gt; 0</span>. Open the bracket: <span class=\"eq\">p·G − L + p·L &gt; 0</span>. Move L across: <span class=\"eq\">p·(G + L) &gt; L</span>. Divide by G + L, which is positive, so the inequality keeps its direction: <b>p &gt; L / (G + L)</b>. With G = 1,000 and L = 9,000 that is 9,000 / 10,000 = 0.90."},{"h":"Read the formula","p":"The cut-off follows the loss-to-gain ratio. Equal costs: 0.50. Loss 3 times the gain: 0.75. 9 times: 0.90. 19 times: 0.95. In odds form: approve only when the odds of repayment beat L : G, which is 9 to 1 here. A bigger loss means you need more certainty before saying yes."},{"h":"The formula trusts the probability","p":"It assumes p is the true chance. If the model says 0.92 but those applicants really repay 85% of the time, the true expected profit is 10,000 × 0.85 − 9,000 = −500 per loan, not the +200 the model implies. An over-confident model therefore needs a higher raw cut-off. Calibrate first (previous simulation), then apply the formula."},{"h":"On real rows the best cut-off is noisy","p":"On 3,000 applicants the profit curve is jagged, because each loan moves it by +1,000 or −9,000, so its peak sits where luck put it. Over 20 datasets the best cut-off for logistic regression ranged from 0.84 to 0.95 (mean 0.90) and the formula's 0.90 earned 95% of the best profit on average. Use the formula, and use the curve only to check you are not on a cliff."}],"example":{"title":"the cut-off from the money, then seven applicants","setup":"Gain G = 1,000 per repaid loan, loss L = 9,000 per default, rejecting earns 0. A calibrated model gives seven applicants the probabilities of repaying in the first column. For each, work out the expected profit of approving.","table":{"head":["Applicant P(repay)","Expected profit = 1,000·p − 9,000·(1 − p)","Approve?"],"rows":[["0.97","+700","yes"],["0.94","+400","yes"],["0.91","+100","yes"],["0.90","0","indifferent: the break-even"],["0.88","−200","no"],["0.75","−1,500","no"],["0.40","−5,000","no"]]},"walk":["Take p = 0.80. Over 100 such applicants, 80 repay (+80,000) and 20 default (−180,000). Net −100,000, so −1,000 per applicant: <span class=\"eq\">0.80 × 1,000 − 0.20 × 9,000 = 800 − 1,800 = −1,000</span>. Reject.","Take p = 0.95. Over 100 applicants, 95 repay (+95,000) and 5 default (−45,000). Net +50,000, so +500 each: <span class=\"eq\">950 − 450 = +500</span>. Approve.","Simplify for any p: <span class=\"eq\">1,000·p − 9,000·(1 − p) = 1,000·p − 9,000 + 9,000·p = 10,000·p − 9,000</span>. That is a straight line in p, and it crosses zero where 10,000·p = 9,000, so at p = 0.90.","At p = 0.90, out of 100 applicants 90 repay (+90,000) and 10 default (−90,000). Zero. This is the break-even: nine good loans pay for exactly one bad one.","Fill the table with 10,000·p − 9,000: for 0.97 it is 9,700 − 9,000 = +700, for 0.88 it is 8,800 − 9,000 = −200. Every applicant at or above 0.90 is non-negative, every one below is negative.","Other costs, same algebra, <span class=\"eq\">L / (G + L)</span>: loss 1,000 gives 0.50, loss 3,000 gives 0.75, loss 19,000 gives 0.95.","Now break the assumption. The model says 0.92 for a group that really repays 85%. The model's expected profit is 10,000 × 0.92 − 9,000 = +200 per loan, the true one is 10,000 × 0.85 − 9,000 = <b>−500</b>. Approving them loses money."],"result":"Cut-off 0.90. Approve the first three applicants (expected +700, +400 and +100) and reject the rest. The line 10,000·p − 9,000 is the whole decision rule: the sign of the expected profit. If the probabilities are not honest, the line is drawn at the wrong place and the rule fails."},"read":[["Sliders: Profit per repaid loan, Loss per default","The G (100 to 5,000) and L (500 to 20,000) of the formula. Rejecting always earns 0. Moving them does not move your cut-off by itself."],["Slider: Approve if P(repay) is at least","Your cut-off. Starts at 0.5. Click the profit plot, or focus it and press the arrow keys, to move it by 0.01."],["Buttons: Use the formula, Use the best found","The first sets the cut-off to L / (G + L), rounded to 0.01. The second sets it to the best step the slider can show (steps of 0.01) on these 3,000 rows."],["Checkbox: Use an uncalibrated model (naive Bayes)","Same applicants, but the probabilities come from naive Bayes, which is over-confident, instead of logistic regression."],["Table: Decisions against outcomes","Rows: Approve, Reject, All applicants. Columns: Repaid, Defaulted, Money. Approve and repaid earns money, approve and defaulted loses money, reject is 0. The Money column adds up to the total profit."],["Readouts: Total profit, Approval rate, Defaults let through","The result at your cut-off. \"Defaults let through\" also shows them as a share of the approved loans."],["Readouts: Formula, Best cut-off on these rows, Profit at the best cut-off, Best step of the slider (0.01)","The formula's cut-off. The exact peak of the profit curve for these particular applicants (three decimals) and what it earns. And the best step the slider can reach, with its profit."],["Profit curve","Total profit if you approve everyone at or above each cut-off. The zero line is break-even. The grey dashed vertical line is cut-off 0.5, the green line is the formula, the diamond is the exact peak on these rows, the black dot is your cut-off."],["Strip plot: best cut-off found on 20 other datasets","One dot per dataset for logistic regression and for naive Bayes. The black bar is the average and the green line is the formula."],["Button: New applicants","A fresh made-up data set (800 to learn from, 3,000 to test on, about 20% default). The numbers in this lesson are for the data set shown when the page first loads."]],"experiments":[{"do":"Before touching anything, read the readouts and the table at the starting cut-off of 0.5.","see":"Approval rate 93.6%. Defaults let through 445 (15.8% of approved). Total profit −1,642,000. In the table, Approve and Repaid is 2,363 loans (+2,363,000) and Approve and Defaulted is 445 loans (−4,005,000).","why":"At 0.5 you approve everyone whose repay chance is only 50% to 90%. Each is worth a negative amount on average (a 0.80 applicant is −1,000), and each default costs nine times what a repaid loan earns. The model is 83.4% accurate here and still loses money."},{"do":"Answer the <i>Predict first</i> question, or press <i>Use the formula</i>.","see":"Cut-off 0.90. Total profit +797,000. Approval rate 42.2% (1,267 loans). Defaults let through 47 (3.7% of approved).","why":"Of the 1,267 approved, 1,220 repay (+1,220,000) and 47 default (−423,000). Approving everybody would lose 2,830,000, and the 0.5 cut-off loses 1,642,000. Being choosy is worth millions."},{"do":"Press <i>Use the best found</i>. Then move the slider one step right, to 0.91.","see":"Use the best found puts the cut-off at 0.90, the same as the formula: Total profit +797,000. \"Best cut-off on these rows\" reads 0.905 with \"Profit at the best cut-off\" +818,000, and \"Best step of the slider (0.01)\" reads 0.90 (+797,000). At 0.91 the profit is +790,000.","why":"The exact peak at 0.905 lies between two slider steps, and the curve is jagged: with 3,000 rows each loan moves it by +1,000 or −9,000. From 0.90 to 0.905, 59 loans leave (51 repaid, 8 defaulted) for a gain of 21,000. From 0.905 to 0.91, another 48 leave (46 repaid, 2 defaulted) for a loss of 28,000. This is why you should not hunt for the exact peak: differences of 3% are noise."},{"do":"Set <i>Loss per default</i> to 1,000, press <i>Use the formula</i>, and read the table. Repeat for 3,000 and 19,000.","see":"The formula gives 0.50, 0.75 and 0.95. Profit at the formula: +1,918,000 (exact peak 0.545 earns +1,925,000), +1,388,000 (peak 0.769 earns +1,398,000) and +415,000 (peak 0.931 earns +499,000).","why":"The cut-off tracks L / (G + L). For small losses the curve is flat near its top and the formula earns 99.6% and 99.3% of the best profit. At a loss of 19,000 only 655 applicants are approved, so luck matters more and the formula reaches 83% of the best."},{"do":"Set the loss back to 9,000, tick <i>Use an uncalibrated model (naive Bayes)</i> and press <i>Use the formula</i>.","see":"The profit peak moves right, to 0.953 (+783,000), but at the green formula line, 0.90, profit is only +601,000, which is 77% of the peak. 105 defaults get through (6.4% of the 1,651 approved).","why":"Naive Bayes counts income, balance and savings three times, so its probabilities are too extreme. The 214 applicants it scored between 0.88 and 0.92 actually repaid 86% of the time, so each approval had an expected profit of 10,000 × 0.86 − 9,000 = −400. A higher raw cut-off compensates."},{"do":"Look at the strip plot at the bottom.","see":"Logistic regression: 20 dots from 0.84 to 0.95 with average 0.90, centred on the green line. Naive Bayes: 20 dots from 0.91 to 0.98, average 0.96, every one to the right of the line.","why":"For honest probabilities the formula is the target, and the data scatter around it by about ±0.03 from sampling noise alone. For an over-confident model the whole cloud shifts right: the formula is no longer the right cut-off for the raw numbers."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Picture","text":"Draw the straight line <b>expected profit = (G + L)·p − L</b>. It starts at −L when p = 0 and reaches +G when p = 1. The cut-off is where it crosses zero."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"Approve when the odds of repayment beat L : G. Loss 9 times the gain means odds above 9 to 1, which is 90%. Loss 3 times the gain means 3 to 1, which is 75%."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"Raise the loss and the cut-off must rise. If your formula gives 0.10 for gain 1,000 and loss 9,000, you have gain and loss swapped: it would approve almost everyone."},{"tag":"Formula in words","text":"Cut-off = <b>what you lose</b> divided by <b>what you win plus what you lose</b>. At equal stakes, half. As the loss grows the cut-off creeps towards 1 but never reaches it."},{"tag":"Trick","text":"Always check calibration before using the formula. If the probabilities are over-confident, the best raw cut-off sits above L / (G + L): naive Bayes averaged 0.96 over 20 datasets instead of 0.90."},{"tag":"Link to code","text":"<code>model.predict(X)</code> uses a hidden cut-off of 0.5. For a money decision, take <code>predict_proba(X)[:, 1]</code> and compare it with your own number."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"A yes/no model should use 0.5 as the cut-off.\"","right":"0.5 is right only when the two mistakes cost the same. Otherwise the cut-off is L / (G + L). With a loss 9 times the gain, 0.5 loses 1,642,000 on the page's applicants."},{"wrong":"\"Approve when p is above gain / (gain + loss).\"","right":"That is the formula upside down. It gives 0.10 here and would approve almost everybody. The loss goes on top: the more you can lose, the closer to 1 the cut-off moves."},{"wrong":"\"The formula works on any model's raw probabilities.\"","right":"Only on calibrated ones. With raw naive Bayes the formula's 0.90 earned an average of 83% of the best profit over 20 datasets, and 66% on the worst, against 95% for logistic regression."},{"wrong":"\"The best cut-off found on my data is the true best, so ignore the formula.\"","right":"On 3,000 rows the peak is noise: over 20 datasets the best cut-off for the same model ranged from 0.84 to 0.95. The formula, with calibrated probabilities, earned 95% of that on average."},{"wrong":"\"Rejected customers cost nothing, so only defaults matter.\"","right":"A rejected good customer costs the profit you did not make. That opportunity cost is the G in L / (G + L): when G is large the cut-off falls."}],"code":[{"title":"Expected profit and the break-even probability","py":"G, L = 1000, 9000                          # profit per repaid loan, loss per default\nfor p in (0.80, 0.90, 0.95):\n    print(p, round(p * G - (1 - p) * L))   # expected profit of approving one applicant with repay chance p\nprint(\"break-even p:\", L / (G + L))\nfor loss in (1000, 3000, 9000, 19000):\n    print(loss, round(loss / (G + loss), 2))","out":"0.8 -1000\n0.9 0\n0.95 500\nbreak-even p: 0.9\n1000 0.5\n3000 0.75\n9000 0.9\n19000 0.95","walk":[["G, L = 1000, 9000","Assigns two variables in one line. Keeping G and L as names lets you try other costs by changing only this line."],["p * G - (1 - p) * L","The expected profit: probability of repaying times the gain, minus probability of defaulting times the loss. <code>(1 - p)</code> is the default chance."],["round(...)","Rounds to a whole rupee. Without it, floating-point arithmetic would print something like -999.9999999999995."],["L / (G + L)","The break-even probability: the p where the expected profit is exactly zero."],["for loss in (1000, 3000, ...)","Loops over four loss sizes with the same gain, to show how the cut-off follows the ratio."]],"tip":"The expected profit is a straight line in p, so there is exactly one crossing point. That is why a single number works as a rule."},{"title":"Profit against cut-off for a calibrated and an over-confident model","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nfrom sklearn.naive_bayes import GaussianNB\nrng = np.random.RandomState(0); s = rng.normal(size=6000)\nX = s[:, None] + 0.5 * rng.normal(size=(6000, 3))                          # three noisy copies of one signal\ny = (rng.uniform(size=6000) < 1 / (1 + np.exp(-(1.6 + 1.2 * s)))).astype(int)   # 1 = repaid, about 78%\ndef earn(p, t): a = p >= t; return 1000 * np.sum(a & (y[3000:] == 1)) - 9000 * np.sum(a & (y[3000:] == 0))\nfor m in (LogisticRegression(), GaussianNB()):\n    p = m.fit(X[:3000], y[:3000]).predict_proba(X[3000:])[:, 1]\n    ts = np.arange(0.5, 1.0, 0.01); best = ts[np.argmax([earn(p, t) for t in ts])]\n    print(type(m).__name__, \"best cut-off\", round(best, 2), \"profit at 0.90:\", earn(p, 0.90), \"at best:\", earn(p, best))","out":"LogisticRegression best cut-off 0.91 profit at 0.90: 413000 at best: 414000\nGaussianNB best cut-off 0.98 profit at 0.90: 230000 at best: 422000","walk":[["def earn(p, t): ...","Total profit on the held-out rows if you approve everyone with probability at least t. <code>a = p &gt;= t</code> is a True/False array: who is approved."],["a & (y[3000:] == 1)","<code>&amp;</code> means \"and\", element by element: approved <i>and</i> repaid. <code>np.sum</code> counts the True values. Brackets are required because <code>&amp;</code> binds tighter than <code>==</code>."],["1000 * ... - 9000 * ...","Gain times approved-and-repaid minus loss times approved-and-defaulted. This is the Money column of the page's table."],["np.arange(0.5, 1.0, 0.01)","Candidate cut-offs 0.50, 0.51, ... 0.99."],["ts[np.argmax([...])]","<code>np.argmax</code> gives the position of the largest profit, and <code>ts[...]</code> turns the position back into a cut-off: the profit-curve peak."]],"tip":"The calibrated model peaks at 0.91, right next to the formula's 0.90 (profit 413,000 against 414,000). Over-confident naive Bayes peaks at 0.98, and the formula's 0.90 leaves nearly half the profit on the table."},{"title":"Apply the cut-off yourself: predict_proba, not predict","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nrng = np.random.RandomState(0); s = rng.normal(size=6000)\nX = s[:, None] + 0.5 * rng.normal(size=(6000, 3))\ny = (rng.uniform(size=6000) < 1 / (1 + np.exp(-(1.6 + 1.2 * s)))).astype(int)\nm = LogisticRegression().fit(X[:3000], y[:3000])\np = m.predict_proba(X[3000:])[:, 1]                          # P(repay) for each held-out applicant\nfor name, approve in [(\"predict() = cut-off 0.5\", m.predict(X[3000:]) == 1), (\"p >= 0.90\", p >= 0.90)]:\n    rep, dft = np.sum(approve & (y[3000:] == 1)), np.sum(approve & (y[3000:] == 0))\n    print(name, \"approved\", approve.sum(), \"defaults\", dft, \"profit\", 1000 * rep - 9000 * dft)","out":"predict() = cut-off 0.5 approved 2779 defaults 505 profit -2271000\np >= 0.90 approved 853 defaults 44 profit 413000","walk":[["m.predict_proba(X)[:, 1]","Probability of class 1 (repaid) for each applicant. This is the number you compare with your cut-off."],["m.predict(X) == 1","The model's own hard yes/no. It secretly uses the rule \"probability above 0.5\", so it ignores your costs."],["for name, approve in [(..., ...), (..., ...)]","Loops over two (label, True/False array) pairs, so the same profit code scores both decision rules."],["rep, dft = ...","Assigns two counts at once: loans approved that repaid, and loans approved that defaulted."],["approve.sum()","The number of True values: how many applicants were approved."]],"tip":"The same model, with the same probabilities, goes from a loss of 2,271,000 to a profit of 413,000 just by changing the cut-off from 0.5 to the one the costs ask for."}],"check":[{"q":"A repaid loan earns 1,000 and a default loses 9,000. Why should you not approve an applicant whose repay chance is 0.80?","options":["0.80 is below 0.85, the standard minimum","Because 0.80 is closer to 0.5 than to 1","The expected profit is 0.80 × 1,000 − 0.20 × 9,000 = −1,000, a loss on average","The model's accuracy is below 80%"],"answer":2,"why":"Expected profit decides, not the size of the probability. At 0.80, 20 defaults per 100 loans cost 180,000 while 80 repaid loans earn only 80,000."},{"q":"A repaid loan earns 600 and a default loses 1,400. What is the cut-off for P(repay)?","options":["0.70","0.30","0.43","0.50"],"answer":0,"why":"L / (G + L) = 1,400 / (600 + 1,400) = 0.70. The answer 0.30 is the formula upside down, and 0.43 is G divided by L."},{"q":"Your model says 0.92 for a group of applicants who really repay 85% of the time. You use the formula's cut-off 0.90 (G = 1,000, L = 9,000). What happens to this group?","options":["You earn about 200 per loan, as the model predicts","You lose about 9,000 per loan","You lose about 500 per loan, although the model predicted a gain of about 200","You break even"],"answer":2,"why":"The true expected profit is 10,000 × 0.85 − 9,000 = −500. The model thinks it is 10,000 × 0.92 − 9,000 = +200, but its probability is not honest. Calibrate first, or raise the raw cut-off."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain why the loan cut-off is 0.90 and not 0.5, and what must be true of the probabilities for that to work.","model":"Approving an applicant earns 1,000 with chance p and loses 9,000 with chance 1 − p, which is above zero only when p is above 9,000/10,000 = 0.90, so 0.5 would approve many loans that lose money on average. The rule only works if the probabilities are calibrated, because it treats the model's p as the true chance of repayment."},"cheat":"Approve if expected profit p·G − (1 − p)·L is above 0, that is p above L / (G + L). With G = 1,000 and L = 9,000: 0.90 (odds 9 to 1). Cost ratios 1:1, 3:1, 9:1, 19:1 give 0.50, 0.75, 0.90, 0.95. It needs calibrated p: an over-confident model needs a higher raw cut-off. The profit peak on finite data is noisy; the formula is the target."},"decisions/test-set-noise":{"idea":"A test score averages n random rows, so it wobbles around the true accuracy by about <span class=\"eq\">√(p(1 − p) / n)</span>. Two equally good models, scored on their own rows, will differ by chance alone. Only a gap well above that noise means anything.","why":"A 1-point lead on a test set feels like proof that model B is better. At 1,000 rows each it is not: the truly worse model scores higher about 1 time in 4. Shipping the \"winner\" of such a comparison pays for a change that may do nothing. Know how big the noise is before you read a gap.","analogy":"Two students are equally well prepared: each answers any question right with probability 0.85. They sit different papers of 1,000 questions. Their marks still differ by the luck of the questions, by 1.6 percentage points on a typical pair, and by more than 3 points one pair in 20. A longer paper shrinks that luck, but only slowly.","vocab":[["True accuracy p","The accuracy the model would get on all the data there could ever be. You never see it. A test set only estimates it."],["Test accuracy","Right answers divided by n on one test set. It is a random number whose average is p."],["Standard error (SE)","The typical size of the wobble of a test accuracy around p: <span class=\"eq\">√(p(1 − p) / n)</span>. It shrinks as n grows."],["Gap","Test accuracy of B minus test accuracy of A, in percentage points. For separate test sets its SD is <span class=\"eq\">√(SE<sub>A</sub>² + SE<sub>B</sub>²)</span>."],["Middle 95%","The range that holds 95% of the gaps you would see if you repeated the experiment: true gap ± 1.96 SD."],["Reversal","The truly worse model scores higher on the test set. The page counts these as \"Truly worse model scores higher\"."],["Bootstrap interval","Resample the test rows with replacement, recompute the gap, repeat 2,000 times. The middle 95% of those gaps is the interval for the true gap."]],"steps":[{"h":"A test accuracy is an average of coin flips","p":"Each test row is a trial that the model gets right with probability p, its true accuracy. The test accuracy is the number of right answers divided by n. Score the model on a different batch of n rows and you get a different number. So a test accuracy is random, and its average over many batches is p."},{"h":"How big is the wobble?","p":"One right-or-wrong trial with chance p has variance p(1 − p). Averaging n independent trials divides the variance by n. So <span class=\"eq\">SE = √(p(1 − p) / n)</span>. For p = 0.85 and n = 1,000: √(0.1275 / 1,000) = 0.0113, which is 1.13 points. The wobble is largest at p = 0.5 and shrinks towards 0 and 1."},{"h":"The gap wobbles more than either score","p":"If A and B are scored on different rows, their wobbles are independent and the variances add: <span class=\"eq\">SD(gap) = √(SE<sub>A</sub>² + SE<sub>B</sub>²)</span>. For two equal models that is √2 × SE = 1.60 points. About 95% of gaps lie within 1.96 SD of the true gap, so within ±3.1 points when the models are equal."},{"h":"So a small lead can be luck","p":"Say B is truly 1 point better (86% against 85%). The observed gap is centred on +1 with SD 1.57, so it falls below zero with probability about Φ(−1/1.57) = 26% (25.2% from the exact binomial; a tie adds 2.1%). On one pair of test sets the better model wins about 3 times in 4 and shows up behind about 1 time in 4."},{"h":"More rows shrink the noise like 1/√n","p":"SE has √n under the line. 100 times the rows gives 10 times less noise. Halving the noise needs 4 times the rows. A model 1 point behind still scores higher than the better one 38% of the time with 100 rows, 25% with 1,000, 13% with 3,000, 2.2% with 10,000 and 0.2% with 20,000 (exact binomial values; the page estimates them from 1,000 simulated comparisons)."},{"h":"One test set: the bootstrap","p":"In real life you have one test set, not 1,000 comparisons. The bootstrap fakes new test sets by drawing n rows from yours with replacement, recomputing the gap, and repeating 2,000 times. The middle 95% of those gaps is an interval for the true gap. It agrees with the formula: on the default draw −1.2 to +5.1 from the bootstrap, −1.1 to +5.1 from observed score ± 1.96 SE."},{"h":"Reading the interval","p":"If the interval contains 0, this test set cannot say which model is better. That is not the same as \"no difference\". With a true 1-point gap and 1,000 rows each, the interval excludes zero only about 10 times in 100 (theory 9.8%). If the interval excludes zero, you have evidence of a difference. Still ask how big it is and whether it matters."}],"example":{"title":"two equal models, scored on separate rows, by hand","setup":"Models A and B are both truly 85% accurate, and each is scored on its own n test rows. How far do the two scores drift apart by chance? Work out n = 1,000, then see the table for other sizes.","table":{"head":["Rows n","SE of one score = √(0.85 × 0.15 / n)","SD of the gap = √2 × SE","95% band = ±1.96 × SD"],"rows":[["100","3.57 points","5.05 points","±9.90 points"],["1,000","1.13 points","1.60 points","±3.13 points"],["10,000","0.36 points","0.50 points","±0.99 points"],["20,000","0.25 points","0.36 points","±0.70 points"]]},"walk":["One test row is a right-or-wrong trial with p = 0.85, so its variance is p(1 − p) = 0.85 × 0.15 = 0.1275.","The accuracy over n = 1,000 rows averages 1,000 trials: variance 0.1275 / 1,000 = 0.0001275. Its square root is the SE, √0.0001275 = 0.01129, which is <b>1.13 points</b>.","A and B use different rows, so their variances add: 2 × 0.0001275 = 0.000255. The SD of the gap is √0.000255 = 0.01597, which is <b>1.60 points</b> (that is 1.13 × √2).","About 95% of gaps fall within 1.96 × 1.60 = <b>±3.13 points</b>. Two identical models differ by more than 3.1 points about 1 time in 20.","Now make B truly 1 point better. SD(gap) = √(0.86 × 0.14 / 1,000 + 0.85 × 0.15 / 1,000) = √(0.0001204 + 0.0001275) = 1.57 points. The gap is centred on +1, so it is below zero when it is 1 / 1.57 = 0.64 SDs under its centre. A normal table gives 26%. The exact binomial gives 25.2% (and 2.1% for an exact tie).","How many rows make the 95% band only ±1 point for two equal models? Solve 1.96 × √(2 × 0.1275 / n) = 0.01, so n = 2 × 0.1275 × (1.96 / 0.01)² = 9,796. About <b>9,800 rows per model</b>."],"result":"At 1,000 rows each, two equal 85% models differ by more than 3.1 points 1 time in 20, and a model that is truly 1 point better is out-scored about 1 time in 4. Reading a 1-point gap needs about 10,000 rows per model just to bring the noise down to ±1 point. A quick rule near 85% accuracy: SD of the gap ≈ 50 / √n points."},"read":[["Histogram bars","How many of the 1,000 simulated comparisons gave each gap (B minus A, in accuracy points). Each comparison draws two fresh test sets, one per model."],["Dashed bell curve","What theory predicts: a normal curve centred on the true gap with the SD from the formula. The bars should follow it."],["Line at the true gap","The real difference between the models. The bars are centred on it, not on zero."],["Black line at 0","No difference. Bars to the left of it are comparisons where the truly worse model scored higher."],["Shaded band","The middle 95% of the observed gaps."],["Readouts: True gap, Spread of the observed gap (sd)","The real gap and the standard deviation of the 1,000 observed gaps, to compare with the formula."],["Readouts: Middle 95% of the gap, Theory: true gap ± 1.96 sd","The observed band and the one the formula predicts. They should be close."],["Readouts: Truly worse model scores higher, Exact tie","The share of comparisons where the worse model is strictly ahead, and where the scores are exactly equal. If the models are equal, the page shows how often A looks better and how often B does."],["Panel: One test set, with Draw one test set for each model","Draws a single test set for each model and shows the Score of A, Score of B and Observed gap."],["Bottom plot: bootstrap gaps","A histogram of 2,000 resampled gaps. The shaded band is the 95% interval. The diamond is the observed gap. The black line is zero."],["Readouts: 95% bootstrap interval, Interval includes zero?","The two ends of the interval, and a yes or no. Yes means this test set cannot tell the models apart."],["Sliders and New seed","True accuracy of model A and B (50% to 99%), Rows in each test set (100 to 20,000, log scale). New seed repeats the 1,000 comparisons with fresh random draws."]],"experiments":[{"do":"Answer the <i>Predict first</i> question and press a button, or set both accuracies to 85% and the rows to 1,000.","see":"The histogram is centred on 0. Middle 95%: −3.0 to +3.0, theory −3.1 to +3.1. Spread (sd) 1.58 points. A looks better in 50% of comparisons and B in 47%, with 2.8% exact ties.","why":"Equal models have no true gap, yet each wins about half the time. The formula √(2 × 0.85 × 0.15 / 1,000) gives an SD of 1.60 points, and 1.96 times that is 3.1: one comparison in 20 is further out."},{"do":"Set A to 85% and B to 86% (1,000 rows). Read <i>Truly worse model scores higher</i>.","see":"26.5% of the 1,000 comparisons, and 2.1% exact ties. True gap +1.0. Middle 95%: −2.1 to +3.9. Theory: −2.1 to +4.1. Spread 1.59 points.","why":"With an SD of about 1.57 and a true gap of only 1, zero sits just 0.64 SDs below the centre of the bell, so about a quarter of its area lies on the wrong side of zero."},{"do":"With B at 86%, move <i>Rows in each test set</i> to 100, 1,000, 3,000, 10,000 and 20,000.","see":"Spread: 4.80, 1.59, 0.90, 0.51 and 0.35 points. Truly worse model scores higher: 38.4%, 26.5%, 11.4%, 2.8% and 0.3%. The histogram gets about 3 times narrower for every 10 times more rows.","why":"The SD follows 1/√n, and √10 is about 3.2. When the bell is narrower than the true gap, almost none of it crosses zero."},{"do":"Set B to 90% (A at 85%, 1,000 rows).","see":"True gap +5.0. Middle 95%: +2.2 to +7.8. \"Truly worse model scores higher\" shows 0.0%.","why":"The bell is centred 5 points from zero with an SD of 1.5, so zero is more than 3 SDs away. A big true gap is not confused by noise of this size. The trouble is with small gaps, which are the usual ones."},{"do":"Back at A 85% and B 86% with 1,000 rows, press <i>Draw one test set for each model</i> nine times, and read <i>Interval includes zero?</i> each time. The draw shown on load counts as the first.","see":"On load: A 83.8%, B 85.8%, gap +2.0, interval −1.2 to +5.1. Next draws: −0.2 [−3.3, +2.9], −0.8 [−3.9, +2.3], +2.1 [−0.8, +5.1]. Across the first 10 draws the interval includes zero every time, and across the first 40 it does 36 times.","why":"B really is better, yet 2 of the first 4 draws show it behind, and none of the first 10 draws proves anything. A 1-point gap on 1,000 rows is too small to see: the interval excludes zero in only about 1 draw in 10."},{"do":"Press <i>New seed</i> ten times with A 85%, B 86%, 1,000 rows, and watch <i>Truly worse model scores higher</i>.","see":"Over seeds 1 to 10 it wanders between 24.5% and 27.0%.","why":"The page's 1,000 comparisons are themselves a random sample, so the share has its own standard error: √(0.25 × 0.75 / 1,000) = 1.4 points. The exact value is 25.2%. The same noise logic that this page teaches applies to the page's own numbers."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Formula in words","text":"<b>SE = square root of (p times q over n)</b>, where q = 1 − p. For a gap between two models on separate rows, multiply by √2."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"Quadruple the rows to halve the noise. Near 85% accuracy the SD of the gap between two models is about <b>50 / √n</b> points: 1.6 at n = 1,000, 0.5 at n = 10,000."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"Before trusting a gap, compare it with 2 × SD(gap). At 1,000 rows near 85% that is about 3 points. A smaller gap on one test set is not evidence."},{"tag":"Picture","text":"Draw the bell curve of gaps with a mark at the true gap and a line at 0. The area to the left of the line is the chance the worse model wins. Narrower bell (more rows) or bigger true gap means less area."},{"tag":"Trick","text":"\"Interval contains 0\" means \"this test set cannot tell\", not \"the models are equal\". Absence of proof is not proof of absence: with a true 1-point gap, 9 draws in 10 contain 0."},{"tag":"Link to code","text":"<code>rng.binomial(n, p, size)</code> draws \"number correct out of n\" many times in one call. Divide by n to get test accuracies and you can simulate this page in three lines."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"85.0% against 86.0% on 1,000 rows each: B is better.\"","right":"At that size the truly worse model scores higher about 1 time in 4. A 1-point gap is inside the noise."},{"wrong":"\"A bigger test set removes the noise.\"","right":"It only shrinks it like 1/√n. Even with 20,000 rows each, two equal models still differ by up to ±0.7 points in 95% of comparisons."},{"wrong":"\"The interval contains zero, so the models are equal.\"","right":"It means this test set cannot tell them apart. A real 1-point difference leaves zero inside the interval in about 9 draws out of 10 at 1,000 rows."},{"wrong":"\"√(2p(1 − p)/n) is the noise for any two models.\"","right":"It holds when each model has its own test rows, as on this page. If both models are scored on the same rows the gap is usually less noisy, because the rows that are hard for one are hard for the other. See the McNemar simulation."},{"wrong":"\"The standard error tells me how far my model is from the truth.\"","right":"It only describes the random wobble from using n rows. A test set that does not look like future data adds a separate error that no formula here measures."}],"code":[{"title":"Standard error of one score and of the gap, for four test sizes","py":"import numpy as np\np = 0.85                                    # true accuracy of both models\nfor n in (100, 1000, 10000, 20000):\n    se = np.sqrt(p * (1 - p) / n)           # sd of ONE model's test accuracy\n    gap = np.sqrt(2) * se                   # sd of the gap between two equal models on separate test sets\n    print(n, f\"{100 * se:.2f} {100 * gap:.2f} {196 * gap:.2f}\")   # in points; 196 = 100 x 1.96 gives the 95% band","out":"100 3.57 5.05 9.90\n1000 1.13 1.60 3.13\n10000 0.36 0.50 0.99\n20000 0.25 0.36 0.70","walk":[["np.sqrt(p * (1 - p) / n)","The standard error: the square root of p(1 − p) divided by n. Note the division by n sits <i>inside</i> the root, which is where 1/√n comes from."],["np.sqrt(2) * se","Two independent scores: variances add, so the SD is √2 times larger. This is the \"different test sets\" case."],["100 * se","Converts a fraction (0.0113) to percentage points (1.13), the unit the page uses."],["f\"{100 * se:.2f}\"","An f-string: the expression inside the braces is computed and shown with 2 decimals (<code>:.2f</code>)."],["196 * gap","100 × 1.96 × gap: the half-width of the 95% band in points."]],"tip":"Each time n is 10 times bigger, the numbers shrink by about 3.16 (the square root of 10). Compare rows 1,000 and 10,000."},{"title":"Simulate 100,000 comparisons of a truly better model","py":"import numpy as np\nrng = np.random.RandomState(0)\nn, reps = 1000, 100000\na = rng.binomial(n, 0.85, reps) / n        # accuracy A scores on each of reps separate test sets\nb = rng.binomial(n, 0.86, reps) / n        # model B is truly 1 point better, on its own test sets\ngap = 100 * (b - a)                        # observed gap in points\nprint(\"sd\", gap.std().round(2), \"middle 95%\", np.percentile(gap, [2.5, 97.5]).round(1).tolist())\nprint(\"worse model ahead\", (gap < 0).mean().round(3), \"tie\", (gap == 0).mean().round(3))","out":"sd 1.57 middle 95% [-2.1, 4.1]\nworse model ahead 0.256 tie 0.021","walk":[["np.random.RandomState(0)","A random generator with a fixed seed, so you get the same numbers every run."],["rng.binomial(n, 0.85, reps)","Draws <code>reps</code> random counts, each the number of right answers out of n = 1,000 when each is right with chance 0.85. That is one whole test set per count."],["/ n","Turns each count into an accuracy between 0 and 1."],["np.percentile(gap, [2.5, 97.5])","The values below which 2.5% and 97.5% of the gaps fall: the ends of the middle 95%."],["(gap < 0).mean()","<code>gap &lt; 0</code> is a True/False array; its mean is the fraction of True: the share of comparisons where the worse model scored higher."]],"tip":"The exact binomial answer is 0.2524. This run gives 0.256 and other seeds give 0.251 to 0.256: a simulation has its own noise, as the page does."},{"title":"Bootstrap interval for one pair of test sets","py":"import numpy as np\nrng = np.random.RandomState(7)\na = (rng.rand(1000) < 0.85).astype(int)         # 1 = model A is right on this row; A has its own 1,000 rows\nb = (rng.rand(1000) < 0.86).astype(int)         # model B has its own 1,000 rows\ngaps = [100 * (b[rng.randint(0, 1000, 1000)].mean() - a[rng.randint(0, 1000, 1000)].mean()) for _ in range(2000)]\ng = 100 * (b.mean() - a.mean())\nse = 100 * np.sqrt(a.mean() * (1 - a.mean()) / 1000 + b.mean() * (1 - b.mean()) / 1000)\nprint(\"observed gap\", round(g, 1), \"bootstrap interval\", np.percentile(gaps, [2.5, 97.5]).round(1).tolist())\nprint(\"formula interval\", round(g - 1.96 * se, 1), round(g + 1.96 * se, 1))","out":"observed gap 1.0 bootstrap interval [-2.1, 3.9]\nformula interval -2.0 4.0","walk":[["(rng.rand(1000) < 0.85).astype(int)","1,000 random numbers in [0, 1), compared with 0.85. Each is below it with probability 0.85, so True means \"model right on this row\". <code>astype(int)</code> turns True/False into 1/0."],["rng.randint(0, 1000, 1000)","1,000 random row numbers drawn <i>with replacement</i>: some rows appear twice, some not at all. This is the resample."],["b[rng.randint(...)].mean()","Indexing by a list of row numbers picks those rows. The mean of the 1/0 values is the accuracy on the resample."],["for _ in range(2000)","Repeat 2,000 times. The underscore means \"I do not need the loop counter\"."],["np.percentile(gaps, [2.5, 97.5])","The middle 95% of the 2,000 resampled gaps: the bootstrap interval."]],"tip":"The bootstrap and the formula (observed gap ± 1.96 SE) agree here, as they did on the page. The bootstrap earns its keep for statistics with no simple formula, such as AUC or profit."}],"check":[{"q":"Model B has a true accuracy of 86% and model A 85%. Each is scored on its own 1,000 rows. Why can A score higher than B?","options":["Model A must have been trained on more data","The test sets must have overlapped","Each test accuracy wobbles by about 1.1 points around the true value, which is as large as the 1-point difference","Accuracy is not a reliable metric"],"answer":2,"why":"A test accuracy is an average over a random sample of rows, so it differs from the true accuracy by an amount of the order of √(p(1 − p)/n) = 1.1 points. The gap between two such scores has SD about 1.6, easily larger than 1 point."},{"q":"A model has true accuracy 0.80 and is scored on 400 test rows. What is the standard error of its test accuracy?","options":["2 points","4 points","8 points","0.1 points"],"answer":0,"why":"SE = √(0.8 × 0.2 / 400) = √(0.16 / 400) = √0.0004 = 0.02, which is 2 points."},{"q":"You want to halve the width of the 95% band for the gap between two equal models, currently ±3 points at 1,000 rows each. What do you do?","options":["It cannot be changed: the band depends only on the accuracy","Use 4 times as many rows (4,000 each)","Use 10 times as many rows (10,000 each)","Use 2 times as many rows (2,000 each)"],"answer":1,"why":"The band is proportional to 1/√n, so halving it needs √n twice as big, which means n four times as big: 4,000 rows each gives about ±1.6 points."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain why an 86% model can lose to an 85% model on a test set, and what makes the problem smaller.","model":"A test accuracy is an average over a finite random set of rows, so it wobbles around the true accuracy by about √(p(1−p)/n), and the gap between two models wobbles by about 1.4 times that, which at 1,000 rows (1.6 points) is bigger than a 1-point true difference. More rows shrink the wobble like 1/√n, so you need about four times the rows to halve it."},"cheat":"Test accuracy has SE = √(p(1−p)/n). The gap between two models on separate rows has SD √(SE_A² + SE_B²), about √2 × SE for equals; 95% of gaps lie within ±1.96 SD (±3.1 points at 85%, n = 1,000). Quadruple n to halve it. A true 1-point gap is reversed about 1 time in 4 at 1,000 rows. One test set: bootstrap interval; containing 0 means \"cannot tell\"."},"decisions/mcnemar":{"idea":"When two models are scored on the <b>same</b> test rows, only the rows where they <b>disagree</b> can show which is better. McNemar's test counts those rows (b for A, c for B) and asks whether such a lopsided split could come from a fair coin.","why":"A one-point accuracy gap on one test set can be luck. If you crown model A on luck, you retrain, re-audit and redeploy for no gain, or you throw away a model that was just as good. Looking only at the disagreements gives a sharper answer from the same rows than comparing two accuracies as if they were unrelated.","analogy":"Two students sit the same 200-question exam. Questions both get right, or both get wrong, tell you nothing about who is stronger. Only the questions where exactly one of them is right matter. If the students are equally able, each of those questions is a coin toss for who wins it. Your job: decide whether 12 wins to 3 could be a fair coin.","vocab":[["Paired comparison","Both models are scored on the same test rows, so every row gives a pair of verdicts (A right or wrong, B right or wrong). The test uses that pairing."],["b and c","<b>b</b> = rows where only A is right. <b>c</b> = rows where only B is right. Together they are the <i>disagreements</i> (statisticians call them discordant pairs)."],["Null hypothesis","The boring assumption you try to refute: the two models are equally good. The test asks how surprising your data would be if that were true."],["p-value","If the models were equally good, the chance of a split at least as lopsided as yours, in either direction. Small (below 0.05 by habit) means a fair coin rarely does this. It is <b>not</b> the probability that A is better."],["Exact binomial test","Add up fair-coin probabilities for every split at least as lopsided as yours. \"Exact\" because it uses the real coin-flip counts, not an approximation."],["Chi-square version","A shortcut formula, <span class=\"eq\">(|b − c| − 1)² / (b + c)</span>, compared with a chi-square table (1 degree of freedom). The −1 is the continuity correction. It matches the exact answer well once b + c is a few dozen."],["Bootstrap interval","Resample the test rows with replacement, recompute A minus B each time, keep the middle 95%. It shows how big the gap could plausibly be, which a p-value does not."]],"steps":[{"h":"Two models, one test set","p":"Models A and B each predict every test row, and their accuracies differ by, say, 0.8 points. A different test set would give a different gap, sometimes with the sign reversed. So the question is not \"which number is bigger?\" but \"could this gap be luck?\". Because both models saw <b>the same rows</b>, we can answer it far more sharply than by comparing two separate accuracies."},{"h":"Sort every row into one of four boxes","p":"Each row has a pair of verdicts, so the test set becomes a 2 × 2 table: <b>both right</b>, <b>only A right (b)</b>, <b>only B right (c)</b>, <b>both wrong</b>. The four counts add up to n, the number of test rows."},{"h":"The gap lives in two boxes","p":"Accuracy of A = (both right + b) / n. Accuracy of B = (both right + c) / n. Subtract: <span class=\"eq\">A − B = (b − c) / n</span>. \"Both right\" cancels, and \"both wrong\" is in neither. The whole gap is decided by b and c. The rows where the models agree are bystanders."},{"h":"Assume the models are equally good","p":"This is the null hypothesis. On a row where the models disagree, it is then equally likely that A is the right one or B is. So each disagreement is a fair coin flip, and b, the number A wins, behaves like heads in b + c flips: <span class=\"eq\">b ~ Binomial(b + c, ½)</span>. Agreeing rows involve no coin at all."},{"h":"The exact p-value","p":"If the coin is fair, how often would a split be at least as lopsided as yours? The chance of exactly k heads in n flips is <span class=\"eq\">C(n, k) / 2ⁿ</span>. Add the tail on the small side, then double it, because \"B much better\" would have been just as surprising: <span class=\"eq\">p = 2 · P(X ≤ min(b, c))</span>. If b = c the answer is 1."},{"h":"The chi-square shortcut","p":"Adding hundreds of binomial terms by hand was painful, so people used a bell-curve approximation: compare <span class=\"eq\">(|b − c| − 1)² / (b + c)</span> with a chi-square table. The −1 repairs the fact that counts move in whole steps. For 31 against 23 both methods give 0.341. For small counts such as 12 against 3 they differ in the third decimal (0.035 and 0.039), so prefer the exact value."},{"h":"Add an interval for the size","p":"A p-value says whether the gap is distinguishable from zero, not how big it is. The paired bootstrap resamples the test rows (each row keeps both verdicts), recomputes A minus B 2,000 times and keeps the middle 95%. If the interval excludes zero, it agrees with p below 0.05. Its width shows which gaps you cannot rule out."},{"h":"What the test can and cannot say","p":"Small p: the gap is probably not luck. Large p: you <i>cannot tell</i>, which is not the same as \"equal\". The same 55 : 45 split gives p = 0.39 on 110 disagreements and p = 0.037 on 550. The test needs both models scored on the same rows, and it does not apply to cross-validation folds (see the CV t-test page)."}],"example":{"title":"two models, 200 test applicants, solved by hand","setup":"Model A (logistic regression) and model B (another classifier) are scored on the same 200 loan applicants in a test set. Right or wrong is judged against what the applicant actually did. The 2 × 2 table:","table":{"head":["","B right","B wrong","Total"],"rows":[["A right","150 (both right)","12 = b (only A right)","162"],["A wrong","3 = c (only B right)","35 (both wrong)","38"],["Total","153","47","200"]]},"walk":["Accuracies: A = 162/200 = <b>81.0%</b>, B = 153/200 = <b>76.5%</b>. The gap is 4.5 points, which equals (b − c)/n = 9/200. The 150 and the 35 never entered the subtraction.","Assume the models are equally good. The b + c = 15 disagreements are 15 fair-coin flips and A won 12 of them. How often does a fair coin win 12 or more out of 15?","The chance of exactly k wins is C(15, k) / 2¹⁵, and 2¹⁵ = 32,768. By symmetry, \"12 or more\" has the same chance as \"3 or fewer\". The counts C(15, 0..3) are 1, 15, 105, 455, which add up to 576.","One tail: 576 / 32,768 = 0.0176. Either model could have been the lucky one, so double it: <b>p = 0.0352</b>.","Chi-square check: (|12 − 3| − 1)² / 15 = 64 / 15 = 4.27, and the chi-square table (1 degree of freedom) gives p = 0.039. Close, but with only 15 disagreements the exact 0.035 is the one to quote.","Why pairing helps: an unpaired test treats 81.0% and 76.5% as results on two different groups of 200 rows. Its standard error is 4.08 points (z = 1.10, p = 0.27). The paired standard error uses only the disagreements: <span class=\"eq\">√(b + c − (b − c)² / n) / n</span> = √(15 − 0.405) / 200 = 1.91 points, about half.","Bootstrap: resample the 200 rows 2,000 times and keep the middle 95% of A minus B: <b>+1.0 to +8.5 points</b>. It excludes zero, in line with p = 0.035.","How fragile? Suppose B had also been right on one of the 35 \"both wrong\" rows. Then b = 12, c = 4 and p = 0.077. A single row separates \"significant\" from \"not\", so treat a p-value near 0.05 as borderline, not as a verdict."],"result":"Exact p = 0.0352 (chi-square 0.0389). A is 4.5 points better on these rows, with interval +1.0 to +8.5, so luck is an unlikely explanation. But the case rests on 15 rows: with b = 12 and c = 4, p = 0.077."},"read":[["The 2 × 2 table, rows \"A right\" and \"A wrong\", columns \"B right\" and \"B wrong\"","Every test row lands in exactly one cell. Top left is both right, bottom right is both wrong."],["The two highlighted cells, \"b: A right, B wrong\" and \"c: B right, A wrong\"","The only cells that enter the test. The other two are shown so you can see how many rows are being ignored."],["Orange bars in \"If the models were equally good, each disagreement is a coin flip\"","Splits at least as lopsided as yours, in either direction. Their heights add up to the exact p-value. Blue bars are less lopsided splits."],["Solid and dashed vertical lines in that chart","The solid line sits at b and the dashed line at c. The further both are from the middle of the bell, the smaller the p-value."],["Hover text \"A wins x of y\"","The chance, if the models were equally good, of exactly that split. It is one bar, not a tail, so it is much smaller than p."],["Exact McNemar p-value","The two-sided binomial p-value, to 4 decimals. A tiny value reads \"below 0.0001\": small, but never exactly zero."],["Chi-square statistic and p from the chi-square","The shortcut with continuity correction. Compare it with the exact p-value: they agree to 2 or 3 decimals once b + c is large."],["A minus B","The accuracy gap in points, which equals (b − c) / rows × 100."],["Bootstrap histogram, with the diamond, the zero line and \"95% interval\"","How A minus B (accuracy points) varies when the test rows are resampled. If the zero line lies inside the interval, the rows cannot separate the models."],["\"Use: What-if table\"","Type b, c and the number of rows directly. The rows where the models agree are split 88% both right and 12% both wrong, which the test ignores anyway."]],"experiments":[{"do":"Load the page and leave everything alone (Two real classifiers, 1,000 test rows, k = 25). Read the table, then hover over the bar at 31.","see":"The table shows 798 both right, b = 31, c = 23, 148 both wrong. A scores 82.9% and B 82.1%. The exact p-value is 0.3409 and the chi-square p is 0.3408. The hover reads \"A wins 31 of 54: chance 6.03%\". The bootstrap interval is −0.7 to +2.2.","why":"946 rows agree and appear in no bar. Among 54 fair flips you expect 27 wins with a spread of about ±3.7, so 31 is only 1.1 spreads away: ordinary. The orange bars cover 0 to 23 and 31 to 54 wins and add up to 0.34."},{"do":"Drag \"k for model B\" down to 1, then up to 100.","see":"At k = 1, B falls to 74.2% against A's 82.9%: b = 137, c = 50, the p-value reads \"below 0.0001\" (it is about 1.5 × 10⁻¹⁰) and the interval is +6.1 to +11.2. At k = 100, B is 81.8%, b = 34, c = 23, p = 0.185 and the interval (−0.3 to +2.6) contains zero.","why":"A 1-neighbour vote copies the single nearest training applicant, noise included, so it loses clearly and the 187 disagreements are lopsided. With 25 or 100 neighbours the vote is smooth and B behaves like A, so wins split almost evenly."},{"do":"Put k back at 25 and slide \"Test rows\" from 200 up to 5,000, noting the p-value as you go.","see":"p wanders instead of falling steadily: about 0.39 at 200 rows, 0.14 at 700, 0.34 at 1,000, 0.56 at 2,000, 0.21 at 3,000. It first drops below 0.05 near 4,000 rows (0.038) and is 0.006 at 5,000.","why":"The true gap is about one point or less. b − c grows slowly while its noise grows like √(b + c), so only thousands of rows lift the gap clear of the noise. The slider adds rows to the same table, so neighbouring stops share most of their rows."},{"do":"Press \"New data\" four times at 1,000 rows and k = 25, reading the p-value each time.","see":"The four new tables give p = 1.000 (19 against 19), 0.087 (36 against 22), 0.880 (23 against 21) and 1.000 (25 against 24). None is below 0.05.","why":"New training rows and new test rows move b and c by a few either way. p = 1 simply means b = c exactly: nothing to see. It does not prove the models are equal."},{"do":"Switch \"Use\" to \"What-if table\", press \"b = 60, c = 50\", then press \"Same proportions, 5 times the rows\".","see":"First: 783 both right, 107 both wrong, A 84.3% against B 83.3%, gap +1.0 point, p = 0.3909, chi-square statistic 0.74, interval −1.1 to +3.0. After the press, b = 300, c = 250 on 5,000 rows: the gap is still +1.0, but p = 0.0366 and the interval is +0.1 to +1.9.","why":"The ratio is unchanged but the number of coin flips went from 110 to 550. The z-score <span class=\"eq\">(|b − c| − 1) / √(b + c)</span> rose from 0.86 to 2.09. Same gap, five times the evidence, because noise shrinks like 1/√n."},{"do":"From \"b = 60, c = 50\" on 1,000 rows, press \"Add 1,000 agreeing rows\" twice.","see":"p stays exactly 0.3909 and the chi-square statistic stays 0.74. Accuracies rise (86.2% and 85.7% at 2,000 rows, then 86.8% and 86.4% at 3,000), the gap shrinks to +0.5 then +0.3 points, and the interval narrows to −0.5 to +1.6, then −0.4 to +1.0.","why":"Agreeing rows enter neither b nor c, so they carry no information about which model is better. They only dilute the gap and shrink its interval by the same factor, so the verdict does not change."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Picture","text":"Two tally columns. Make a mark for every row where only A is right and for every row where only B is right. Ignore all other rows. Then ask: is this tally lopsided enough that a fair coin would rarely do it?"},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"The square root of the chi-square statistic is a z-score: <span class=\"eq\">(|b − c| − 1) / √(b + c)</span>. Above about 2 means p below about 0.05. Check: 31 v 23 gives 0.95 (p = 0.34); 12 v 3 gives 2.07 (p = 0.039); 300 v 250 gives 2.09 (p = 0.037)."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"The z-score grows like √(b + c). At the same proportions, doubling z needs 4 times the disagreements: b = 60, c = 50 has z = 0.86, and reaching z above 2 took 5 times as many (300 v 250), not a few more rows."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"The four cells must add up to the number of test rows, and swapping A and B must leave p unchanged (only the sign of the gap flips). If b + c = 0 there is nothing to test."},{"tag":"Trick","text":"Large p means \"cannot tell\", not \"equal\". Read the interval as well: −0.7 to +2.2 on the default data still allows A to be 2 points better."},{"tag":"Link to code","text":"<code>binomtest(b, b + c, 0.5).pvalue</code>: successes, trials, null probability. The whole exact test is that one call."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"p = 0.34 means there is a 34% chance that A is better, or that the models are equal.\"","right":"p is worked out <i>assuming</i> the models are equal. It is the chance of a split this lopsided under that assumption. It is not the probability that either claim is true."},{"wrong":"\"The 946 rows where both models agree are most of my evidence, so I should keep them.\"","right":"Keep them for reporting accuracy, but for the test they count for nothing. Add 1,000 more agreeing rows and p does not move."},{"wrong":"\"p above 0.05 means the two models are equally good.\"","right":"It means this test set cannot tell. On the default data the interval still runs from −0.7 to +2.2 points, and on 4,000 rows of the same table the p-value is 0.038."},{"wrong":"\"I can run McNemar on any two accuracy numbers.\"","right":"It needs both models scored on the <b>same</b> rows, so that each row has a pair of verdicts. Two accuracies from different test sets have no b and c."},{"wrong":"\"A significant p-value means the gap is big enough to matter.\"","right":"Significance says the gap is not luck, not that it is large. A 0.3-point gap can be significant on 50,000 rows. Look at the interval, and at the profit it implies."}],"code":[{"title":"From b and c to the p-value, exact and chi-square","py":"from scipy.stats import binomtest, chi2\nb, c = 12, 3                                    # b: only A right, c: only B right\nexact = binomtest(b, n=b + c, p=0.5).pvalue     # A wins b of b + c fair-coin flips, two-sided\nstat = (abs(b - c) - 1) ** 2 / (b + c)          # chi-square statistic with continuity correction\nprint(\"exact p =\", round(exact, 4))\nprint(\"chi-square =\", round(stat, 3), \" p =\", round(chi2.sf(stat, df=1), 4))\nprint(\"widget what-if 60 v 50:\", round(binomtest(60, 110).pvalue, 4), \" 300 v 250:\", round(binomtest(300, 550).pvalue, 4))","out":"exact p = 0.0352\nchi-square = 4.267  p = 0.0389\nwidget what-if 60 v 50: 0.3909  300 v 250: 0.0366","walk":[["binomtest(b, n=b + c, p=0.5)","Runs the exact binomial test: <code>b</code> \"successes\" (A wins) out of <code>n = b + c</code> trials (the disagreements), against a fair coin <code>p = 0.5</code>. It is two-sided by default."],[".pvalue","The result object holds several things; <code>.pvalue</code> is the number you want."],["(abs(b - c) - 1) ** 2 / (b + c)","The chi-square statistic. <code>abs</code> because the sign does not matter, <code>- 1</code> is the continuity correction, <code>** 2</code> squares it."],["chi2.sf(stat, df=1)","<code>sf</code> is the survival function: the chance of a value at least this large, which is the p-value. <code>df=1</code> because there is one comparison (b against c)."],["binomtest(60, 110)","Leaving out <code>p</code> keeps the default 0.5. 60 + 50 = 110 disagreements."]],"tip":"With only 15 disagreements the shortcut is off by 0.004. With 550 it is not: for 300 v 250 the chi-square p is 0.0367 against 0.0366 exact."},{"title":"From two models' predictions to the 2 × 2 table","py":"from sklearn.datasets import make_classification\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nfrom sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nfrom scipy.stats import binomtest\nX, y = make_classification(n_samples=1800, n_features=6, n_informative=2, n_redundant=0, flip_y=0.15, weights=[0.2, 0.8], random_state=1)\nXtr, Xte, ytr, yte = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=1000, random_state=0)\na_ok = LogisticRegression().fit(Xtr, ytr).predict(Xte) == yte   # True where model A is right\nb_ok = KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=25).fit(Xtr, ytr).predict(Xte) == yte\nb, c = int((a_ok & ~b_ok).sum()), int((~a_ok & b_ok).sum())\nprint(\"both right\", int((a_ok & b_ok).sum()), \"| b\", b, \"| c\", c, \"| both wrong\", int((~a_ok & ~b_ok).sum()))\nprint(\"accuracy A\", a_ok.mean(), \"B\", b_ok.mean(), \"| exact p =\", round(binomtest(b, b + c).pvalue, 3))","out":"both right 816 | b 32 | c 20 | both wrong 132\naccuracy A 0.848 B 0.836 | exact p = 0.126","walk":[["test_size=1000","Hold out exactly 1,000 rows as the test set; both models are scored on these same rows, which is what makes the comparison paired."],[".predict(Xte) == yte","Compares each prediction with the true label and gives an array of True (right) and False (wrong), one entry per test row. That array is all the test needs from a model."],["a_ok & ~b_ok","<code>&</code> is element-wise AND, <code>~</code> is NOT. So this is True exactly where A is right and B is wrong: the rows counted by <b>b</b>."],["~a_ok & b_ok","True where only B is right: the count <b>c</b>."],[".sum()","Adding up True and False counts the Trues (True = 1). <code>int(...)</code> only tidies the printout."],["a_ok.mean()","The mean of True/False values is the share of Trues, which is the accuracy."]],"tip":"The accuracies differ by 1.2 points, but the exact p-value is 0.126: a gap of that size on 52 disagreements is not unusual for a fair coin."},{"title":"A paired bootstrap interval for the accuracy gap","py":"import numpy as np\nd = np.r_[np.ones(12), -np.ones(3), np.zeros(185)]     # +1: only A right, -1: only B right, 0: tie\nrng = np.random.RandomState(0)\nrows = rng.randint(0, len(d), size=(2000, len(d)))      # 2000 resamples of the 200 row numbers\ngaps = d[rows].mean(axis=1) * 100                       # A minus B in accuracy points, per resample\nprint(\"observed gap:\", round(d.mean() * 100, 1))\nprint(\"95% interval:\", np.percentile(gaps, [2.5, 97.5]).round(1).tolist())","out":"observed gap: 4.5\n95% interval: [1.0, 8.5]","walk":[["np.r_[np.ones(12), -np.ones(3), np.zeros(185)]","Glues three arrays end to end: one number per test row, equal to A's verdict minus B's (1 − 0, 0 − 1, or a tie). Their mean is the accuracy gap. Storing one number per row keeps both verdicts together, which is what \"paired\" means."],["rng.randint(0, len(d), size=(2000, len(d)))","A 2000 × 200 table of random row numbers drawn <i>with replacement</i>. Each line is one resampled test set of the same size."],["d[rows]","Fancy indexing: looks up the verdict of every drawn row, giving a 2000 × 200 table."],[".mean(axis=1) * 100","<code>axis=1</code> averages along each line, giving 2000 gaps. Times 100 turns them into points."],["np.percentile(gaps, [2.5, 97.5])","The middle 95%: cut off 2.5% at each end."]],"tip":"Resample the rows, never b and c separately; otherwise you break the pairing."}],"check":[{"q":"Two models are scored on the same 500 test rows. They agree on 460 rows and disagree on 40. Which rows does McNemar's test use?","options":["Only the rows both models get wrong","All 500 rows","Only the 40 rows where they disagree","Only the 460 rows where they agree"],"answer":2,"why":"The accuracy gap is (b − c) / n, and b + c = 40 are the only rows that can make b and c differ. Rows where both are right or both wrong cancel out."},{"q":"b = 8 and c = 2. Using the fair-coin counts C(10, k) / 2¹⁰ for k = 0, 1, 2 (which are 1, 10, 45 over 1,024), what is the exact two-sided p-value?","options":["0.109","0.055","0.022","0.80"],"answer":0,"why":"One tail is (1 + 10 + 45) / 1,024 = 56 / 1,024 = 0.0547. Either model could have been the lucky one, so double it: 0.109. The value 0.055 is the one-sided mistake of forgetting to double."},{"q":"A comparison has b = 60 and c = 50 on 1,000 rows, with p = 0.39. You add 2,000 more rows on which both models agree. What happens to the exact p-value?","options":["It halves","It falls below 0.05","It stays 0.39","It rises towards 1"],"answer":2,"why":"Agreeing rows add nothing to b or c. The accuracy gap shrinks (from 1.0 to 0.3 points) and so does its interval, but p depends only on b and c."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain to a friend why McNemar's test ignores most of the test set, and what it asks of the rows it keeps.","model":"Rows where both models are right or both are wrong cannot show which model is better, so the test throws them away and counts only the rows where exactly one is right. If the models were equally good, each such row would be a fair coin flip, so it asks how rarely a fair coin gives a split as lopsided as b to c; if that chance is below 0.05, the gap is probably real."},"cheat":"Same test rows for both models. b = only A right, c = only B right. Equal models make each disagreement a fair coin: exact p = 2 · P(X ≤ min(b, c)), X ~ Binomial(b + c, ½); in scipy, binomtest(b, b + c).pvalue. Quick check: (|b − c| − 1) / √(b + c) above 2 means p below 0.05. Agreeing rows add nothing. Large p: \"cannot tell\"."},"decisions/cv-t-test":{"idea":"Cross-validation gives J accuracy differences between two models, but they come from the same few rows, so they are not J independent looks. A plain paired t-test pretends they are and finds winners that do not exist. Nadeau and Bengio's correction fixes the standard error.","why":"Repeating K-fold feels like more evidence, so the plain t-test grows more confident with every repeat, even for two models that are equally good. On this page, 5 folds repeated 5 times name a winner in 21.7% of comparisons instead of 5%. You would ship or reject models on noise.","analogy":"A pollster asks 300 voters a question, then asks the same 300 again with the questions shuffled, five times over. She now holds 1,500 answers but only 300 opinions, and the same few swing voters sway every round. Quoting the margin of error for 1,500 people would be dishonest. Repeated K-fold on 300 rows is the same: many scores, one sample of 300 applicants.","vocab":[["K-fold, repeats R","Split the rows into K folds, train on K − 1 of them, test on the one left out, and rotate. Repeat with a fresh shuffle R times. Each model gets J = K × R test scores."],["Fold difference d","Accuracy of A minus accuracy of B on one test fold. The t-test works on the list d₁ … d_J."],["Naive paired t-test","<span class=\"eq\">t = mean(d) / (s / √J)</span>, where s is the standard deviation of the d's, looked up with J − 1 degrees of freedom. It is valid only if the d's are independent."],["Standard error (SE)","How much the average difference would wobble if you repeated the whole experiment on fresh data. A t-test divides the average by its SE."],["n_test / n_train","Rows in one test fold divided by rows in one training set. In K-fold it equals <span class=\"eq\">1 / (K − 1)</span>: 0.25 for K = 5, 0.11 for K = 10."],["False alarm","The test says \"different\" (p below 0.05) when the models are equally good. An honest test does this 5% of the time."],["Corrected resampled t-test","Nadeau and Bengio's version: the same t-test, but the variance of the average is <span class=\"eq\">s² (1/J + n_test/n_train)</span> instead of <span class=\"eq\">s² / J</span>."],["A/A test","Compare two equally good models on fresh data many times. A sound procedure flags about 5%. The page does exactly this, 300 times."]],"steps":[{"h":"The question","p":"You have models A and B and n = 300 applicants. K-fold cross-validation scores both models on every row, one fold at a time, giving K differences d = acc<sub>A</sub> − acc<sub>B</sub>. Repeating the whole K-fold R times with fresh shuffles gives J = K × R differences. Is their average really different from zero?"},{"h":"The paired t-test","p":"Compute the average d̄ and the standard deviation s of the d's. Averaging J independent numbers shrinks the wobble by √J, so the standard error is s / √J. Then <span class=\"eq\">t = d̄ / (s / √J)</span>, read in a t-table with J − 1 degrees of freedom. A large |t| gives a small p."},{"h":"The hidden assumption: independence","p":"The √J is right only if the J numbers are independent looks. Inside one K-fold pass they nearly are for these two models (fold-to-fold correlation about 0; the naive test flags only 3.7% at R = 1). Repeats break it. Every repeat re-scores the same 300 rows with models that share 75% of their training rows. On the first comparison (K = 5, R = 5) the models disagree 40 times, but on only 19 different rows, and each row always favours the same model."},{"h":"What goes wrong","p":"Each extra repeat raises J, so the naive SE keeps shrinking, but no new rows arrived, so the true wobble stalls and t inflates. Measured over 300 fresh data sets (K = 5): the real spread of d̄ is 0.98 points at R = 1, 0.70 at R = 5, 0.64 at R = 10. The naive SE claims 0.95, 0.43, 0.30. False alarms: 3.7%, 21.7%, 38.7%."},{"h":"The correction","p":"Nadeau and Bengio (2003): <span class=\"eq\">Var(d̄) ≈ s² (1/J + n_test/n_train)</span>. The first term is the usual one. The second is a <b>floor</b> that more repeats cannot shrink: it stands for having only n rows. Same J − 1 degrees of freedom. Equivalent shortcut: <span class=\"eq\">t<sub>corr</sub> = t<sub>naive</sub> / √(1 + J · n_test/n_train)</span>."},{"h":"How big is the correction?","p":"With K = 5, n_test/n_train = 0.25, so the naive t is divided by √(1 + J/4): 1.5 at J = 5, 1.87 at J = 10, 2.69 at J = 25, 3.67 at J = 50. More repeats mean a bigger divisor, which cancels the inflation that the repeats caused. With K = 10 the ratio is 0.11 and J = 100 gives 3.48."},{"h":"What the page shows, and what it does not","p":"On equal models the corrected test flagged 0 to 2 of 300 comparisons in every setting tried, against a 5% target. That is on the safe side. It also means the test may miss real differences, and this page does not measure that: it only counts false alarms. The correction is an approximation, not an exact result."},{"h":"Which test when","p":"One test set, both models fitted once: McNemar or a paired bootstrap on the predictions (previous lesson). Only cross-validation scores available: the corrected resampled t-test. Plain paired t-test on repeated CV folds: avoid, because it looks rigorous and misleads. Choose the test that counts each row once, or that pays for re-using rows."}],"example":{"title":"five folds, one comparison, by hand","setup":"Two logistic regressions (regularisation C = 1 and C = 0.1) are compared by 5-fold cross-validation on 300 made-up applicants: each fold tests on 60 rows and trains on 240. The models are equal by design, so any \"winner\" is a false alarm. In fold j, A got d<sub>j</sub> more of the 60 test rows right than B: <b>2, 1, 0, 1, 1</b>. (On a freshly loaded page set K = 5 and R = 1, then press \"Next comparison\" 19 times to reach comparison 20.)","table":{"head":["Fold j","A minus B (rows right)","A minus B (points)","d − mean (rows)","(d − mean)²"],"rows":[["1","+2","+3.33","+1","1"],["2","+1","+1.67","0","0"],["3","0","0.00","−1","1"],["4","+1","+1.67","0","0"],["5","+1","+1.67","0","0"],["mean, sum","1.0","+1.67","0","2"]]},"walk":["Average: d̄ = (2 + 1 + 0 + 1 + 1) / 5 = 1.0 row, which is 1/60 = +1.67 points.","Spread: s² = Σ(d − d̄)² / (J − 1) = 2 / 4 = 0.5, so s = 0.707 rows (1.18 points).","Naive: SE = s / √J = 0.707 / 2.236 = 0.316. Then t = 1.0 / 0.316 = <b>3.16</b> with 4 degrees of freedom, and p = <b>0.034</b>. The page's callout reads \"t = 3.16, p = 0.034 (significant)\". A false alarm.","Corrected: n_test / n_train = 60 / 240 = 0.25. The variance factor is 1/J + 0.25 = 0.2 + 0.25 = 0.45 instead of 0.2. SE = 0.707 × √0.45 = 0.474, so t = 1.0 / 0.474 = <b>2.11</b> and p = <b>0.103</b>.","The shortcut gives the same: t<sub>corr</sub> = 3.16 / √(1 + 5 × 0.25) = 3.16 / 1.5 = 2.11.","A third opinion: pool the 300 rows. The two models disagreed on 7 of them and A won 6 (b + c = 7, b − c = 5, matching the 5 extra rows A got right). McNemar exact: p = 2 × (1 + 7) / 128 = <b>0.125</b>.","Corrected (0.103) and McNemar (0.125) both say \"not enough evidence\". Only the naive test says \"winner\": it counts five folds as five independent looks at what is really a 7-row disagreement."],"result":"From the same five numbers: naive p = 0.034 (a false alarm), corrected p = 0.103, McNemar on the pooled rows p = 0.125. The only change between the first two is that the variance factor 1/J = 0.2 becomes 1/J + n_test/n_train = 0.45."},"read":[["Blue dots in \"One comparison, fold by fold\"","One dot per fold: A minus B in accuracy points. With K = 5 a fold has 60 rows, so one row is 1.67 points and the dots sit on a grid. The first K dots are repeat 1, the next K repeat 2, and so on."],["Orange line and dark zero line","Orange is the average of all the dots, dark is \"no difference\". The naive t-test measures the distance between them in units of s / √J."],["Callout \"Naive t-test: t = …, p = …\" and \"Corrected: …\"","The two verdicts on this one comparison. \"(significant)\" appears when p is below 0.05. Since the models are equal, either is a false alarm."],["Buttons \"← Earlier comparison\" and \"Next comparison →\"","Step through the 300 comparisons. The card heading shows which one you are on."],["Readout \"Fold differences per test, J = K × R\"","How many numbers go into each t-test."],["Readouts \"Naive t-test: p < 0.05\" and \"Corrected t-test: p < 0.05\"","How many comparisons were flagged. For equal models about 5% is honest. The page colours a rate above 10% as bad."],["Readout \"Average of A minus B\"","Close to zero by design (for example −0.05 ± 0.04 points), confirming that the models really are equal on average."],["Readout \"Rows where A and B differ\"","About 3%. A handful of rows decide every comparison, which is why repeats add so little."],["The two p-value histograms and the dashed line","Each bar counts comparisons with p in a slice of width 0.05. The dashed line, at 300 / 20 = 15, is what an honest test gives: flat. The highlighted first bar is p below 0.05, the false alarms."],["Table \"False alarms\"","Count and rate of p below 0.05 for each test, next to the \"about 5%\" an honest test gives. The class notes quote 44% and 2% from a different experiment (fair-coin labels on 200 rows). The page's first run gives 21.7% and 0.0% at K = 5, R = 5, and 45.3% and 0.7% at K = 10, R = 10."]],"experiments":[{"do":"Load the page and leave K = 5, R = 5. Read the table \"False alarms\".","see":"Naive t-test: 65 of 300 (21.7%). Corrected t-test: 0 of 300 (0.0%). The average of A minus B is −0.05 ± 0.04 points and A and B differ on 3.1% of rows.","why":"The models are equal by design, so every flag is wrong. The naive test errs 4.3 times as often as its 5% promise (21.7 / 5)."},{"do":"Set K = 5 and R = 1, then raise R to 2, 5 and 10.","see":"Naive flags: 11 of 300 (3.7%), 19 (6.3%), 65 (21.7%), 116 (38.7%). Corrected: 2, 1, 0, 1.","why":"Repeats add numbers but no rows. The naive SE shrinks like 1/√J while the real wobble of the average levels off near 0.65 points (measured: 0.98, 0.81, 0.70, 0.64 for R = 1, 2, 5, 10; naive SE 0.95, 0.66, 0.43, 0.30). A shrinking denominator pumps t up."},{"do":"Set R = 5 and move K from 2 to 10.","see":"Naive false alarms climb: 46 of 300 at K = 2 (15.3%), 65 at K = 5 (21.7%), 87 at K = 10 (29.0%). Corrected stays at 0, 0 and 2.","why":"J = K × R grows with K, so the naive SE shrinks further. The correction divides t by √(1 + J/(K − 1)): 3.32 at K = 2, 2.69 at K = 5, 2.56 at K = 10, so it follows the problem."},{"do":"Look at the two histograms at K = 5, R = 5.","see":"Naive: the first bar (p below 0.05) holds 65 comparisons against the dashed line at 15, then 30, 25, 17. Corrected: the first five bars hold 0, 4, 2, 1, 3 and the bars on the right are full (35 in the 0.85 to 0.90 bar).","why":"An honest test gives p-values spread evenly, a flat histogram at 15. The naive test piles them at the left (false alarms). The corrected test tilts the other way: it is cautious, which protects against false alarms and may also hide real gaps."},{"do":"Press \"Next comparison →\" three times to reach comparison 4, then once more. Compare with comparison 1.","see":"Comparison 1: average −0.27 points, naive p = 0.603, corrected 0.847. Comparison 4: average +1.20 points, naive p = 0.013 (significant), corrected 0.328. Comparison 5: average −1.53, naive p = 0.000, corrected 0.139. In comparison 5, 15 of the 25 dots are below zero and only 2 above.","why":"The dots look like a consistent signal, but each repeat re-scores the same rows with almost the same models, so the same few rows push every repeat the same way. The naive test counts them 5 times."},{"do":"Press \"Run 300 comparisons again\" three times.","see":"At K = 5, R = 5 the naive counts are 59, 68 and 56 of 300 (the first run was 65) and the corrected counts are 0, 1 and 0.","why":"Each run uses 300 fresh data sets, so a count moves by about ±7 by chance. But the naive count stays near 4 times the 15 an honest test gives, and the corrected one never comes close to it."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Formula in words","text":"<b>Corrected t = naive t ÷ √(1 + J × n_test/n_train).</b> For K = 5 and J = 25 the divisor is 2.69. Use it to check any CV t-test you read."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"In K-fold, n_test/n_train = 1/(K − 1): 1 for K = 2, 0.25 for K = 5, 0.11 for K = 10. For huge J the corrected variance floors at s² × n_test/n_train, so with K = 5 repeats can never push the SE below 0.5 s."},{"tag":"Picture","text":"Under the 25 dots there are only 300 rows, and about 21 of them ever disagree (page default, first 100 comparisons). That is the real sample, whatever J is. Count rows, not folds."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"Repeats polish the estimate, not the evidence. They smooth out the luck of one random split, so d̄ is steadier, but they cannot remove the uncertainty of having only n rows."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"To audit any comparison procedure, run it on a model against an equal twin, many times. Honest means about 5% flagged and a flat p-value histogram."},{"tag":"Link to code","text":"<code>stats.ttest_1samp(d, 0)</code> is the naive test. Divide its <code>.statistic</code> by <code>√(1 + J · n_test/n_train)</code> and recompute p with J − 1 degrees of freedom."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"25 fold differences give 24 degrees of freedom of evidence.\"","right":"The degrees of freedom assume independent numbers. These 25 rest on 300 rows, about 21 of which ever disagree. Far less independent information than 25."},{"wrong":"\"More repeats of K-fold make the comparison more trustworthy.\"","right":"They make the average difference steadier, not more certain: the rows are the same. Naive false alarms go 3.7%, 6.3%, 21.7%, 38.7% for R = 1, 2, 5, 10."},{"wrong":"\"Test folds do not overlap, so the fold differences are independent.\"","right":"Within one pass the dependence can be small. On this page, two folds of one 5-fold pass had a correlation near 0 (average −0.01 over 300 data sets), and the naive test flagged 3.7%. The dependence arrives with repeats: the average differences of two repeats on one data set correlate at 0.42. The correction covers both."},{"wrong":"\"The corrected test flagged 0 of 300, so it is perfect.\"","right":"It is conservative here: 0 to 2 of 300 against a 5% target of about 15. A test that rarely says \"different\" may miss real differences, and the page does not measure that."}],"code":[{"title":"Naive and corrected t from five fold differences","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\nd = np.array([2, 1, 0, 1, 1]) / 60              # A minus B accuracy in each fold (60 test rows per fold)\nJ, ratio = len(d), 60 / 240                     # number of differences, n_test / n_train\nt_naive = d.mean() / np.sqrt(d.var(ddof=1) / J)\nt_corr = d.mean() / np.sqrt(d.var(ddof=1) * (1 / J + ratio))\nfor name, t in [(\"naive\", t_naive), (\"corrected\", t_corr)]:\n    print(name, \"t =\", round(t, 2), \"p =\", round(2 * stats.t.sf(abs(t), df=J - 1), 3))\nprint(\"one-line shortcut:\", round(t_naive / np.sqrt(1 + J * ratio), 2))","out":"naive t = 3.16 p = 0.034\ncorrected t = 2.11 p = 0.103\none-line shortcut: 2.11","walk":[["np.array([2, 1, 0, 1, 1]) / 60","The five fold differences in rows, divided by the 60 test rows of a fold so they become accuracy differences."],["d.var(ddof=1)","The sample variance s². <code>ddof=1</code> divides by J − 1 instead of J, the usual choice for a sample."],["d.var(ddof=1) / J","The naive variance of the average, s² / J. Its square root is the standard error."],["(1 / J + ratio)","The only change in the corrected version: the factor <code>1 / J</code> becomes <code>1 / J + n_test/n_train</code>."],["stats.t.sf(abs(t), df=J - 1)","Upper-tail probability of the t-distribution with J − 1 degrees of freedom. Doubled because a large negative t is just as surprising as a large positive one."]],"tip":"With ten folds of the same 300 rows, ratio = 30 / 270 = 0.11 instead of 0.25, so the extra term and the correction are smaller."},{"title":"Count the false alarms: 100 comparisons of two equal models","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression as LR\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import RepeatedStratifiedKFold as RSKF, cross_val_score as cvs\nhits = np.zeros(2)                                                  # false alarms: [naive, corrected]\nfor seed in range(100):                                             # 100 fresh data sets\n    r = np.random.RandomState(seed); Z = r.randn(300, 3)            # Z[:, 0] = hidden strength, Z[:, 1:] = noise\n    X, y = Z[:, :1] + .6 * Z[:, 1:], r.binomial(1, 1 / (1 + np.exp(-1.5 - 1.2 * Z[:, 0])))\n    a, b = [cvs(LR(), X[:, [j]], y, cv=RSKF(n_splits=5, n_repeats=5, random_state=seed)) for j in (0, 1)]\n    t = stats.ttest_1samp(a - b, 0).statistic                       # naive t on 25 differences\n    hits += [abs(t) > 2.064, abs(t) / np.sqrt(1 + 25 / 4) > 2.064]  # 2.064 = 97.5th percentile of t, 24 df\nprint(\"false alarms out of 100: naive\", int(hits[0]), \"corrected\", int(hits[1]))","out":"false alarms out of 100: naive 35 corrected 7","walk":[["Z[:, :1] + .6 * Z[:, 1:]","Two columns that are noisy copies of the same hidden strength (income and salary slip). They are exchangeable, so a model using column 0 and a model using column 1 are equally good by symmetry: an A/A test."],["X[:, [j]]","Double brackets keep a 2-D table with one column, which scikit-learn requires. A is column 0, B is column 1."],["RSKF(n_splits=5, n_repeats=5, random_state=seed)","Repeated stratified 5-fold, giving 25 test scores per model. The fixed <code>random_state</code> makes both models see exactly the same 25 splits, so the scores are paired."],["cvs(LR(), ..., cv=...)","<code>cross_val_score</code> returns one accuracy per split. Subtracting the two arrays gives the 25 fold differences d."],["stats.ttest_1samp(a - b, 0).statistic","The naive t of the differences against 0."],["abs(t) / np.sqrt(1 + 25 / 4) > 2.064","Corrected t, using t<sub>corr</sub> = t / √(1 + J · n_test/n_train) with J = 25 and ratio 1/4. 2.064 is the 5% cut-off for 24 degrees of freedom."]],"tip":"Over 500 data sets the same code gives about 37% naive and 3% corrected; 100 is noisy (the corrected 7 is within chance of 5)."},{"title":"How much does the correction divide t by?","py":"import numpy as np\nprint(\"K R J n_test/n_train divide_t_by\")\nfor K, R in [(5, 1), (5, 5), (5, 10), (10, 10)]:\n    J, ratio = K * R, 1 / (K - 1)               # in K-fold, n_test / n_train = 1 / (K - 1)\n    print(K, R, J, round(ratio, 3), round(float(np.sqrt(1 + J * ratio)), 2))","out":"K R J n_test/n_train divide_t_by\n5 1 5 0.25 1.5\n5 5 25 0.25 2.69\n5 10 50 0.25 3.67\n10 10 100 0.111 3.48","walk":[["1 / (K - 1)","In K-fold one fold is tested and K − 1 are used for training, so n_test / n_train = (n/K) / (n(K − 1)/K) = 1/(K − 1)."],["J = K * R","Total fold differences: K per pass, R passes."],["np.sqrt(1 + J * ratio)","The divisor from t<sub>corr</sub> = t<sub>naive</sub> / √(1 + J · ratio). It grows with J, which is what cancels the inflation caused by repeats."],["round(float(...), 2)","<code>float</code> turns the NumPy number into a plain number so it prints as 1.5, not np.float64(1.5)."]],"tip":"Compare with the page: at K = 5 the naive false alarms were 3.7%, 21.7% and 38.7% for R = 1, 5 and 10, the same order as the divisors 1.5, 2.69 and 3.67 grow."}],"check":[{"q":"Why does the plain paired t-test flag too many equally good models when K-fold is repeated?","options":["It treats the K × R differences as independent, but they re-use the same rows, so its standard error is too small","The folds have different sizes, which biases the average","Repeating K-fold changes the models, so they are no longer equally good","The t-distribution cannot be used with accuracy numbers"],"answer":0,"why":"The naive standard error s/√J assumes J independent looks. Repeats re-score the same rows with almost the same models, so the true wobble stays put while s/√J keeps shrinking and t inflates."},{"q":"You use K = 10 folds repeated R = 3 times (J = 30). By what number does the corrected test divide the naive t?","options":["About 2.92","About 2.08","1, no change","About 4.33"],"answer":1,"why":"n_test/n_train = 1/9 = 0.111. Then 1 + 30 × 0.111 = 4.33, and the divisor is its square root, 2.08. 2.92 is what K = 5 would give; 4.33 forgets the square root."},{"q":"Same two equal models, same 300 rows, K = 5. You raise the repeats from R = 5 to R = 10. What happens to the naive test's false-alarm rate?","options":["It drops to 0%","It rises, from 21.7% to 38.7% on this page","It falls towards 5%","It stays near 21.7%"],"answer":1,"why":"J doubles from 25 to 50, so the naive SE shrinks by about 1.4 times, but no new rows were added. The false alarms grow."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain why a paired t-test over repeated K-fold differences gives false alarms, and what the Nadeau and Bengio correction adds.","model":"The fold differences all come from the same rows and from models trained on mostly the same rows, so they hold far less independent information than J numbers, yet the plain t-test divides by √J as if they were independent. The correction adds n_test/n_train to the variance, a floor that repeating cannot shrink, so the standard error stays honest."},"cheat":"Repeated K-fold gives J = K × R differences d that re-use the same rows. The naive paired t uses SE² = s²/J and cries wolf (21.7% with K = 5, R = 5 on equal models). Nadeau and Bengio: SE² = s²(1/J + n_test/n_train), with n_test/n_train = 1/(K − 1); same as t_naive ÷ √(1 + J · n_test/n_train). With one test set, use McNemar instead."},"decisions/group-metrics":{"idea":"One model and one cut-off can treat two groups of applicants very differently. Compute approval rate, share of good applicants approved, share of defaulters approved, precision and accuracy for each group: equal accuracy can hide unequal errors, and equalising one figure moves the others.","why":"An overall accuracy of 71.4% hides that group A scores 74.8% and group B 66.1%, and that A is approved 65.5% of the time but B only 41.9%. The model never saw the group, yet outcomes differ. You find out only by measuring each group. Which rates to equalise is a policy choice, and every choice costs something elsewhere.","analogy":"One pass mark for two exam halls. Hall A passes 65% of its students, hall B 42%. Four separate questions: how many capable students passed? how many weak ones slipped through? of everyone who passed, how many are capable? how many passed in all? Lowering the mark in hall B lifts the first two answers and drops the third, all at once.","vocab":[["Positive, approve","Here the positive class is \"repays\". Approving an applicant means predicting positive: the model's P(repay) is at least the cut-off."],["tp, fp, fn, tn","Counts within one group. <b>tp</b>: approved and repaid. <b>fp</b>: approved and defaulted. <b>fn</b>: rejected but would have repaid. <b>tn</b>: rejected and would have defaulted."],["Approval rate","<span class=\"eq\">(tp + fp) / n</span>. The share of the group that gets a yes. Bottom of the fraction: everyone in the group."],["TPR, \"good approved\"","<span class=\"eq\">tp / (tp + fn)</span>. Of the applicants who would repay, the share we approved. Bottom: the good applicants."],["FPR, \"defaulters approved\"","<span class=\"eq\">fp / (fp + tn)</span>. Of the applicants who would default, the share we approved. Bottom: the defaulters."],["Precision","<span class=\"eq\">tp / (tp + fp)</span>. Of the applicants we approved, the share who repay. Bottom: the approved."],["Base rate","The share of a group that would repay (the \"Would repay\" row: 80.2% for A, 68.0% for B). Different base rates are why not everything can be made equal."],["Proxy","A column that is not the sensitive attribute but carries information about it. Dropping the sensitive column does not remove its information while proxies remain."]],"steps":[{"h":"One score, one cut-off, two groups","p":"The model gives each applicant a score, P(repay). The rule: approve if the score is at least a cut-off t. One t for everybody looks equal in form. The group label is not one of the six columns the model uses (income, balance, savings, debt, history, loan size), so the model never sees it."},{"h":"Four cells per group","p":"Split the test applicants by group and fill a confusion table for each: tp, fp, fn, tn. Everything on the page is a ratio of these four counts. The \"All\" column is just the groups' cells added together, so it can look healthy while one group is not."},{"h":"Five ratios, five denominators","p":"Approval rate (tp + fp) / n. TPR tp / (tp + fn). FPR fp / (fp + tn). Precision tp / (tp + fp). Accuracy (tp + tn) / n. Before computing any, ask \"out of whom?\". Most mix-ups, such as taking FPR for 1 − precision (26.4% against 8.0% for group A), are a wrong denominator."},{"h":"Why one cut-off gives different rates","p":"The groups have different score histograms and different base rates (80.2% and 68.0% would repay). B's scores sit lower, so the same vertical line removes more of B's histogram. B is approved less often although the rule is identical."},{"h":"Accuracy mixes two kinds of error","p":"Accuracy = 1 − (fp + fn) / n. A false positive (approving a defaulter) costs 9,000 here, a false negative (rejecting a good applicant) loses a 1,000 gain. Two groups can share an accuracy with very different mixes: with B's cut-off at 0.63, B scores 74.7% and A 74.8%, yet 9.9% of B's applicants are approved defaulters against 5.2% of A's."},{"h":"You cannot equalise everything","p":"Precision = <span class=\"eq\">TPR·π / (TPR·π + FPR·(1 − π))</span>, where π is the base rate. Give B the same TPR and FPR as A and keep B's base rate, and B's precision is 85.8%, not A's 92.0%. So moving B's cut-off to match one figure moves the others. Chouldechova (2017) and Kleinberg et al. (2016) prove versions of this."},{"h":"Dropping the group column is not enough","p":"The group label never enters the model, yet scores differ, because the columns differ by group. On this table a logistic regression guesses the group from the six columns with AUC 0.67 (0.5 is a coin flip). Dropping any single column leaves 0.66 to 0.67. Dropping income, balance and savings together, three noisy copies of one hidden strength, still leaves 0.60. The information is spread over correlated columns."},{"h":"Measure, then decide","p":"The page measures. It does not say which figure to equalise: that is a policy and legal choice. Matching approval rates keeps both groups at about 65%, but B's defaulters approved rise from 12.3% to 33.7% and overall profit falls from +535,000 to −552,000. Rules on group-specific cut-offs in real lending differ by country and are outside this page."}],"example":{"title":"two groups, one cut-off, by hand","setup":"The page as loaded: 6,000 made-up test applicants, 3,609 in group A and 2,391 in group B, scored by a logistic regression that does not see the group. Approve if P(repay) is at least 0.80. Positive = repays. Profit is +1,000 for each repaid loan approved and −9,000 for each default approved. The confusion counts (the last row is group B after moving its cut-off to 0.63):","table":{"head":["Group, cut-off","tp","fp","fn","tn","n"],"rows":[["A, 0.80","2,174","189","719","527","3,609"],["B, 0.80","908","94","717","672","2,391"],["B, 0.63","1,255","236","370","530","2,391"]]},"walk":["<b>Approval rate</b> = (tp + fp) / n. A: (2,174 + 189) / 3,609 = 2,363 / 3,609 = <b>65.5%</b>. B: (908 + 94) / 2,391 = 1,002 / 2,391 = <b>41.9%</b>.","<b>Good approved (TPR)</b> = tp / (tp + fn). A: 2,174 / 2,893 = <b>75.1%</b>. B: 908 / 1,625 = <b>55.9%</b>. The bottom is the applicants who would repay.","<b>Defaulters approved (FPR)</b> = fp / (fp + tn). A: 189 / 716 = <b>26.4%</b>. B: 94 / 766 = <b>12.3%</b>. The bottom is the applicants who would default.","<b>Precision</b> = tp / (tp + fp). A: 2,174 / 2,363 = <b>92.0%</b>. B: 908 / 1,002 = <b>90.6%</b>. Nearly equal, although B is approved 24 points less often: precision cannot see who was turned away.","<b>Accuracy</b> = (tp + tn) / n. A: 2,701 / 3,609 = <b>74.8%</b>. B: 1,580 / 2,391 = <b>66.1%</b>. All together: 4,281 / 6,000 = 71.35%, which the page shows as 71.4%.","<b>Profit</b> = 1,000 · tp − 9,000 · fp. A: 2,174,000 − 1,701,000 = +473,000. B: 908,000 − 846,000 = +62,000. Total +535,000. A group earns money exactly when its precision beats 9,000 / (1,000 + 9,000) = 0.90, so B's 90.6% only just clears it.","Now press \"Accuracy\": B's cut-off moves to 0.63 (third row). Accuracy = (1,255 + 530) / 2,391 = <b>74.7%</b>, level with A's 74.8%. (The page's note adds that 0.30 also matches, at 74.8%.) But the mix differs. Approved defaulters: 236 / 2,391 = 9.9% of B's applicants against 189 / 3,609 = 5.2% of A's. Rejected good applicants: 15.5% against 19.9%. FPR 30.8% against 26.4%, precision 84.2% against 92.0%."],"result":"At one cut-off of 0.80, A is approved 65.5% of the time and B 41.9%, yet precision is nearly equal (92.0% and 90.6%). Equal accuracy (74.8% and 74.7%, with B at 0.63) hides 9.9% of B's applicants being approved defaulters against 5.2% of A's. The overall 71.4% describes neither group."},"read":[["Histograms \"Group A: predicted P(repay)\" and \"Group B: predicted P(repay)\"","Each bar counts applicants whose predicted P(repay) falls in a slice of width 0.05, with \"repaid\" and \"defaulted\" stacked. Group B's bars sit further left."],["Dark vertical line in each histogram","The cut-off. Applicants to the right of it are approved, those to the left are rejected."],["\"Approve if P(repay) is at least\": \"One threshold for everyone\" or \"One per group\"","The second option is how the page lets you treat the two groups differently."],["Table rows \"Applicants\" and \"Would repay\"","The size of each group and its base rate."],["Rows \"Approval rate\", \"Good approved (TPR)\", \"Defaulters approved (FPR)\", \"Precision\", \"Accuracy\"","The five ratios, for group A, group B and all applicants. Check the denominators with the cheat card."],["Rows \"Profit\" and \"Defaults approved\"","Profit is +1,000 per repaid loan approved and −9,000 per default approved. \"Defaults approved\" is the raw count fp."],["Column \"B − A\"","B's figure minus A's in points. It turns green when the gap is under 1 point. Green only means \"equal\", not \"good\"."],["Buttons under \"Make one measure equal\"","A keeps its cut-off. The page searches B's cut-off for the value where B's figure is closest to A's, switches to \"One per group\", and you read what happened to the other rows. For \"Accuracy\" a note under the table lists every cut-off that matches."],["Readouts \"Overall accuracy\", \"Overall approval rate\", \"Overall profit\"","The same figures for all applicants together. They can look fine while a group is not."]],"experiments":[{"do":"Load the page and read the table at the starting cut-off of 0.80. Compare the two histograms.","see":"Approval rate 65.5% for A and 41.9% for B. Good approved 75.1% and 55.9%, defaulters approved 26.4% and 12.3%, precision 92.0% and 90.6%, accuracy 74.8% and 66.1%. Overall accuracy 71.4%. In the \"Would repay\" row A is 80.2% and B 68.0%.","why":"B's histogram sits further left, so the same line cuts away more of it. B's approvals are rarer but about as clean (precision 90.6%), which is why precision alone would not show the gap."},{"do":"Press \"Approval rate\" under \"Make one measure equal\".","see":"The page switches to \"One per group\" and sets B's cut-off to 0.60. Approval rates are 65.5% and 65.0%. But defaulters approved become 26.4% against 33.7%, precision 92.0% against 83.4%, B's profit −1,025,000, and overall profit moves from +535,000 to −552,000.","why":"To approve 23 points more of B, the cut-off drops to 0.60. Those extra applicants have a lower P(repay), and a group loses money once precision falls below 0.90. B's precision is now 83.4%."},{"do":"Press \"Good applicants approved (TPR)\", read the table, then press \"Defaulters approved (FPR)\" and read it again.","see":"TPR button: B's cut-off 0.65. TPR 75.1% against 75.6%, but approval 65.5% against 60.7%, defaulters approved 26.4% against 29.0%, precision 92.0% against 84.7%. FPR button: cut-off 0.67. FPR 26.4% against 26.6%, but TPR 75.1% against 72.9%, approval 65.5% against 58.1%, precision 92.0% against 85.3%.","why":"Each button fixes one row and the others stay unequal. B has a lower base rate, so with equal TPR and FPR its precision would fall to 85.8%. Equalising everything at once is not possible with one score."},{"do":"Press \"Precision\" and compare group B's approval rate and the overall profit with the start.","see":"B's cut-off becomes 0.85. Precision is 92.0% against 92.2%, but B's approval rate falls from 41.9% to 34.3%, B's good applicants approved from 55.9% to 46.6%, and overall profit rises from +535,000 to +654,000.","why":"Equal precision is reached by being stricter with B. That removes B's riskiest approvals, so profit goes up, while B's good applicants are turned away more often. Money and equality are different axes."},{"do":"Reload the page, press \"New applicants\" three times, then press \"Accuracy\".","see":"The page's note says B's accuracy equals A's at two cut-offs, 0.33 and 0.59, and that the button used 0.33. Accuracy is 74.1% for A and 74.2% for B, but approval rates are 63.0% and 86.7%, defaulters approved 19.8% and 69.0%, precision 94.2% and 73.8%, overall profit −2,401,000.","why":"Accuracy rises and then falls as the cut-off moves, so B's accuracy equals A's at two cut-offs (0.33 and 0.59 on this table). The button uses the closer match, here the extreme one. Equal accuracy tells you nothing about which errors are made."},{"do":"Reload the page, then press \"New applicants\" four times and read the table at 0.80 each time.","see":"B's approval rate is 24.7, 24.1, 24.7 and 27.5 points below A's (70.6% against 45.9%, 68.4% against 44.3%, 63.0% against 38.3%, 67.3% against 39.8%). B's accuracy is 6 to 12 points below A's every time.","why":"The groups are drawn from different distributions on purpose, so the pattern is built in and repeats on every table. These are measurements on made-up applicants, not claims about any real population. The slides used a different generator (35% in group B, cut-off 0.90), so slide numbers differ from the page."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Trick","text":"For every rate ask <b>\"out of whom?\"</b> Approval rate: everyone in the group. TPR: those who would repay. FPR: those who would default. Precision: those we approved. Accuracy: everyone. Nearly all mix-ups are a wrong denominator."},{"tag":"Formula in words","text":"A group earns money when <b>precision is above loss / (gain + loss)</b>, here 9,000 / 10,000 = 0.90. Check: B's precision 90.6% gave +62,000; at 83.4% B lost 1,025,000."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"Accuracy is the weakest kind of equality: false positives and false negatives can trade against each other. Look at the pair (FPR, TPR), or at all four cells, never at accuracy alone."},{"tag":"Formula in words","text":"<b>Precision = TPR·π / (TPR·π + FPR·(1 − π))</b>. Same TPR and FPR but a lower base rate π means lower precision. A's rates with B's π give 85.8% instead of 92.0%."},{"tag":"Picture","text":"Two histograms, one line each. Sliding B's line changes four numbers at once. Each \"Make one measure equal\" button says: slide B's line until this number matches A's, then read the rest."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"For each group tp + fp + fn + tn must equal n (2,174 + 189 + 719 + 527 = 3,609), and the \"All\" cells must equal A plus B. If a rate does not recompute from the cells, a cell is mislabelled."},{"tag":"Trick","text":"To look for proxies, try to predict the group from the other columns. AUC clearly above 0.5 means the columns carry group information: 0.67 on this table, 0.60 even after dropping income, balance and savings."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"The model never sees the group, so it cannot treat the groups differently.\"","right":"Outcomes differ because the inputs differ by group. Here approval is 65.5% for A and 41.9% for B with the group column absent, and the group can be guessed from the other columns with AUC 0.67."},{"wrong":"\"Equal accuracy means the model is equally good for both groups.\"","right":"Accuracy adds two kinds of error together. At 74.8% and 74.7%, 5.2% of A's applicants and 9.9% of B's are approved defaulters."},{"wrong":"\"Fair means approval rate, TPR, FPR and precision are all equal.\"","right":"With base rates of 80.2% and 68.0% one score cannot do all of that. Each button equalises one figure and moves the rest. Which to equalise is a policy choice, not a statistic."},{"wrong":"\"FPR is the share of approved applicants who default.\"","right":"That is 1 − precision (8.0% for A). FPR is the share of <i>defaulters</i> who were approved (26.4% for A). Different denominators, different numbers."}],"code":[{"title":"Five rates from four counts, per group","py":"def rates(tp, fp, fn, tn):\n    n = tp + fp + fn + tn\n    return {\"approval\": (tp + fp) / n, \"TPR\": tp / (tp + fn), \"FPR\": fp / (fp + tn),\n            \"precision\": tp / (tp + fp), \"accuracy\": (tp + tn) / n}\nfor name, cells in [(\"A\", (2174, 189, 719, 527)), (\"B\", (908, 94, 717, 672))]:\n    print(name, *[f\"{k} {v:.3f}\" for k, v in rates(*cells).items()], \"profit\", 1000 * cells[0] - 9000 * cells[1])","out":"A approval 0.655 TPR 0.751 FPR 0.264 precision 0.920 accuracy 0.748 profit 473000\nB approval 0.419 TPR 0.559 FPR 0.123 precision 0.906 accuracy 0.661 profit 62000","walk":[["def rates(tp, fp, fn, tn)","One function per group. The four counts are all it needs; each rate is a different ratio of them."],["(tp + fp) / n","Approval rate: everyone approved over everyone in the group."],["tp / (tp + fn)  and  fp / (fp + tn)","TPR divides by the good applicants, FPR by the defaulters. Same numerator type, different denominators."],["rates(*cells)","The <code>*</code> unpacks the tuple into four separate arguments."],["f\"{k} {v:.3f}\"","An f-string: insert the name and the value rounded to 3 decimals."],["1000 * cells[0] - 9000 * cells[1]","Profit: +1,000 per tp (cells[0]) and −9,000 per fp (cells[1])."]],"tip":"These are the page's own numbers at 0.80. Paste the third row of the example's table in to reproduce the \"Accuracy\" button."},{"title":"The same rates from rows, using masks","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nr = np.random.RandomState(0); n = 6000\ng = (r.rand(n) < 0.4).astype(int)                                       # 1 = group B; never given to the model\ns = r.randn(n) - 0.6 * g                                                # hidden financial strength, lower on average in B\nX = np.c_[s + .5 * r.randn(n), s + .5 * r.randn(n), s + .6 * r.randn(n)]  # income, balance, savings: noisy copies of s\ny = (r.rand(n) < 1 / (1 + np.exp(-1.5 - 1.2 * s))).astype(int)          # 1 = repaid\nok = LogisticRegression().fit(X, y).predict_proba(X)[:, 1] >= 0.8       # approve if P(repay) >= 0.8, same cut-off for all\nfor k in (0, 1):\n    m = g == k                                                          # mask: the rows of one group\n    print(\"AB\"[k], \"repay\", y[m].mean().round(3), \"approval\", ok[m].mean().round(3), \"TPR\", ok[m & (y == 1)].mean().round(3),\n          \"FPR\", ok[m & (y == 0)].mean().round(3), \"precision\", y[m & ok].mean().round(3))","out":"A repay 0.768 approval 0.511 TPR 0.595 FPR 0.234 precision 0.894\nB repay 0.651 approval 0.317 TPR 0.418 FPR 0.128 precision 0.859","walk":[["g == k","A True/False array, one entry per applicant: True for the rows of group k. Use it to pick out one group."],["ok[m].mean()","<code>ok</code> is True where approved. The mean of True/False values is the share of Trues: the approval rate within the group."],["ok[m & (y == 1)].mean()","Narrow the rows to this group <i>and</i> good applicants, then take the share approved: TPR. The mask chooses the denominator."],["ok[m & (y == 0)].mean()","Same with defaulters: FPR."],["y[m & ok].mean()","Now narrow to the approved rows and take the share who repaid: precision. Mask by the approved, average the outcome."],["predict_proba(X)[:, 1]","The model's P(repay) for each applicant. Column 1 is the probability of class 1 (repaid)."]],"tip":"For brevity the model is scored on its own training rows. The page scores separate test rows, and you should too."},{"title":"Can the group be guessed after the column is dropped?","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score\nr = np.random.RandomState(0); n = 6000\ng = (r.rand(n) < 0.4).astype(int)                                       # 1 = group B: the column we \"dropped\"\ns = r.randn(n) - 0.6 * g                                                # hidden strength, lower on average in B\nX = np.c_[s + .5 * r.randn(n), s + .5 * r.randn(n), s + .6 * r.randn(n)]  # income, balance, savings: noisy copies of s\nfor keep in ([0, 1, 2], [1, 2], [2]):                                   # then drop income, then balance as well\n    auc = cross_val_score(LogisticRegression(), X[:, keep], g, cv=5, scoring=\"roc_auc\").mean()\n    print(\"columns kept\", keep, \"-> AUC for guessing the group:\", round(auc, 3))","out":"columns kept [0, 1, 2] -> AUC for guessing the group: 0.644\ncolumns kept [1, 2] -> AUC for guessing the group: 0.639\ncolumns kept [2] -> AUC for guessing the group: 0.632","walk":[["X[:, keep]","Keeps only the listed columns. The group column <code>g</code> is not in X at all; it is the thing we try to predict."],["cross_val_score(..., cv=5, scoring=\"roc_auc\")","Trains on 4/5 of the rows and scores AUC on the other 1/5, five times, so we measure guessing on unseen applicants."],[".mean()","Average the five AUC scores. AUC 0.5 means a coin flip, 1.0 means perfect."],["keep in ([0, 1, 2], [1, 2], [2])","Drop income, then balance as well. If dropping a column removed its information, AUC would fall to 0.5. It barely moves."]],"tip":"The page's table is not this one, but the lesson is the same: its six columns give AUC 0.67, and 0.60 after dropping income, balance and savings."}],"check":[{"q":"Which rate answers \"of the applicants who would have repaid, how many did we approve?\"","options":["Precision","True positive rate (good applicants approved)","Approval rate","False positive rate (defaulters approved)"],"answer":1,"why":"The bottom of the fraction is \"applicants who would repay\", so it is tp / (tp + fn), the TPR. Precision divides by the approved, FPR by the defaulters, approval rate by everyone."},{"q":"Group B at cut-off 0.80 has tp = 908, fp = 94, fn = 717, tn = 672. What is its false positive rate (share of defaulters approved)?","options":["12.3% (94 / 766)","9.4% (94 / 1,002)","44.1% (717 / 1,625)","3.9% (94 / 2,391)"],"answer":0,"why":"FPR = fp / (fp + tn) = 94 / (94 + 672) = 94 / 766 = 12.3%. 9.4% is 1 − precision, 3.9% divides by everyone, and 44.1% is the share of good applicants rejected."},{"q":"On the page, you lower group B's cut-off until B's approval rate equals A's (65.5%). What happens to B's precision and to overall profit?","options":["Only B's accuracy changes","B's precision falls (90.6% to 83.4%) and overall profit turns negative (+535,000 to −552,000)","B's precision stays the same because the model has not changed","B's precision rises and overall profit rises"],"answer":1,"why":"A lower cut-off approves applicants with a lower P(repay), so a larger share of the approved default. Since a default costs 9 times a repaid loan's gain, profit falls once precision is below 0.90."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain why you should look at error rates for each group and not just overall accuracy, and why you cannot simply make every rate equal.","model":"Overall accuracy averages the groups and mixes false approvals with false rejections, so two groups can share an accuracy while one has 9.9% approved defaulters and the other 5.2%. And when the groups have different base rates, one score cannot give equal approval rate, TPR, FPR and precision at once, so equalising one figure moves the others and which to choose is a policy decision."},"cheat":"Per group, from tp, fp, fn, tn: approval (tp+fp)/n; TPR tp/(tp+fn); FPR fp/(fp+tn); precision tp/(tp+fp); accuracy (tp+tn)/n. Ask \"out of whom?\". Different score histograms and base rates make one cut-off give unequal rates. Equal accuracy can hide unequal errors. Different base rates mean not every rate can be equal. Dropping a column leaves proxies. Which to equalise is policy."}});
